{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals","id":1382668,"title":"I don't understand the purpose of M20 nerfing acolyte contributions to rituals.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-04-19T20:27:18-07:00","id":1382668,"title":"I don't understand the purpose of M20 nerfing acolyte contributions to rituals.","content":"Background on the issue: In 2nd and 3rd/Revised edition, the rules for &quot;Acting in Concert&quot; put forward that every 5 acolytes involved in an effect contribute an automatic success to the end result of the ritual. In M20, it's every 5 acolytes contributing a single die, <i>and</i> (here's the part I don't get) it's capped it at 5 additional dice.<br /> <br /> Let me say that there are plenty of rules in M20 that I disagree with; mostly the sort that, rather than clarifying a previously ambiguous/vague rule, or expanding upon a previously inadequately explained rule, instead just take a clear rule and change it <i>for reasons</i>. The two examples that immediately come to mind are the radical changes to how mind control magick works (what <i>HDYDT</i> calls &quot;Uncanny Influence&quot;); and the second that quickly comes to mind is the fact that potentially every supernatural in the game can now have some kind of countermagick.<br /> <br /> I don't play with these rules; some because I don't like or agree with them (not because they're objectively bad, so much as they're not to my taste), others because I really just <i>can't</i>--I've been running a game for the better part of a decade based mostly on Revised rules, so even if I wanted to incorporate every single rule, I couldn't. There are a handful of important things that have occurred in the plot that would become inexplicable if certain fundamental rules were to just change. <br /> <br /> Nevertheless, even where I disagree with the change, I at least understand the underlying reason for the change, most of which are obvious on their face. Regarding the two examples above, the reason it's very difficult to use Mind to control people without their consent is a product of Satyros Brucato's social politics; I think he subscribes to the school of thought that matters of personal consent and bodily autonomy have been disregarded by our society for far too long and that it would be deeply violating and wrong to invade someone's mind and force them to do things they don't want to do... and so if it's really difficult to do that in-game, players will discard it as a viable strategy, and he'll (Brucato) have the satisfaction of knowing that when people play the game he put his name on, using the rules he wrote, that it'll be nigh impossible for certain things to transpire. The whole 'giving nightfolk countermagick' is even simpler than that: they doesn't want mages pwning other supernatural with incomparable attacks that no one but another mage has an answer for. Again, I don't use these rules, but I understand the policy reason for why they were implemented in the first place.<br /> <br /> But this brings me to the rule at hand about Acolytes contributing to rituals. Switching it from automatic successes to dice doesn't make me feel one way or another--but capping it at a maximum benefit of five is inexplicable to me. I can't explain it with Brucato's politics, and I can't explain it with a balancing issue, because it's probably incredibly difficult to get even 100 or more people together so bought into and knowledgeable about your paradigm that they'd count as &quot;Acolytes&quot; for the purposes of a ritual. And if you've already accomplished that, then, the premise of whatever game you're playing probably relies on that kind of extraordinary occurrence having come to pass. But even from a metaphysical standpoint, the way magick is supposed to work in this world, it doesn't make sense to me that having 20 acolytes help on a ritual is numerically going to be the same as having 1,000 or 10,000. Like, what are we fixing here?<br /> <br /> This is one of those rules that I couldn't change even if I wanted to because there's Big-Time-Plot-Device-Setting-Stuff that happened in my game that's explained by the notion that the rulers of ancient empires lead by people holding themselves out as a gods (or high priests) would have been able to accomplish miraculous feats because of the scale of the fanatical following of their state religions. But I'd still like to understand the <u><b><i>why</i></b></u> here.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T06:21:23-07:00","id":1382734,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals#post1382668\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I'd still like to understand the <u><b><i>why</i></b></u> here.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that the answer may be the Cult Background, which it's limited by more general Background rules:<br /> <br /> The Cult Background comes from the Guide of the Traditions, and there it reached only to 5 like most Backgrounds do (and made no mention of this being any limit to Core ritual rules). M20 ported it &quot;as it is&quot; (Cult doesn't go beyond 5 regardless of the size of your Cult), and this seems to be balanced with the number of successes Acolites can get as per Rituals (both are 5).<br /> <br /> SO, I would say that M20 treats Acolites helping in rituals <b>as </b>the Cult Background (if you want your Acolites to do that, you need to buy/have a Cult in the same vein as you need them as Allies if you want them to have your back in a figth, I think this mechanic was mentioned in Gods and Monsters but not 100% sure)...or at least that it may have been intended to be &quot;balanced&quot; with that specific number","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-20-2020, 09:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T07:31:37-07:00","id":1382748,"title":"","content":"I don't see the why explained in game. To explain the people who have many worshippers or acolytes following them, perhaps thinking of Reality Zones would help. If a small group of devoted followers will only get so far, then having thousands of people believe in what you are doing means you've got a reality zone.<br /> <br /> There may also be some game balance in the reduction of acolyte power. While it's not the same game system, GURPS also limits the power from having a bunch of participants in a magical ritual. If I needed an in-game headcanon, I'd probably go with the scope of control to keep all of those worshippers focused on a ritual is so difficult to maintain that there's a practical number. Beyond that, the risk of someone losing focus and messing up the ritual means mages set that as a limit in their minds. Is that a real-world limit based on the laws of reality that is a consensual belief, is it something hard-coded by the material world, or just mages not being imaginative? That leaves you an out for high ritual with many participants if you want to move beyond RAW (rules as written).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T10:26:30-07:00","id":1382790,"title":"","content":"While obviously none of us can speak for Brucato on this:<br /> <br /> It needed the nerf. I might be something of a rarity, but the cooperative casting rules are some of my favorites to bust on to show how powerful mages really are; esp. since you don't see it in play that much as most PC groups can't effectively use them due to wanted to run a multi-splat cabal instead of sharing enough beliefs to cast together.<br /> <br /> Five freebies for +5 successes to your ritual is <b>mind numbing</b> in terms of how good it is. There's no mechanical drawback, just the narrative issue of maintain your cult; but not having a cult with the old rules was a chump move because... how can you compete with +5 successes like that?<br /> <br /> Moving it to dice does two important things:<br /> <br /> First, the nerf is totally deserved as noted.<br /> <br /> Second, making it dice gives it a different bonus from apprentices and peers helping you, which is good for encouraging different approaches. Cult is no longer a must have for any seriously powerful mage (and as a late addition Background, it's something a lot of the NPCs don't have which makes them much less impressive if you have people that make full use of the team rules). The usefulness of dice also scales with the difficulty of the casting. If you're sitting pretty at Diff 3 for your casting, dice are almost as good as successes, as your cult can easily keep up with your magic. If you're up to a difficulty 9 threshold 2 spell... the magical intricacies and highly delicate working make it that much harder for the cult to help.<br /> <br /> -------<br /> <br /> The cap of 5? That I'm more hit or miss on. Though I do think that baakycalder is right that getting more and more people on the same page is hard enough there's clearly a diminishing returns issue. This is actually the book's position:<br /> <br /> &quot;It\u2019s possible to have a larger cult, of course \u2013 a popular<br /> evangelist might have hundreds of devotees. After a certain<br /> point, though, they can help you only so much.&quot;<br /> <br /> M20 p. 310<br /> <br /> Baakycalder is also right about the idea that having even more people impacts the local Reality Zone, again, this is already a bonus in the text:<br /> <br /> &quot;If you\u2019ve got over 100 people involved, certain vulgar<br /> Effects (summonings, gateways, raisings of the dead,<br /> and so forth) may be considered coincidental if there<br /> are no other witnesses around to contradict that impression.&quot;<br /> <br /> M20 p. 542.<br /> <br /> While it's not exactly changing the local Reality Zone permanently, getting a massive gathering going can turn vulgar w/o witnesses magic into coincidental. That's a huge deal in and of itself.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T12:04:31-07:00","id":1382803,"title":"","content":"I would propose a compromise rule making it so that you have to add 5 people after every 5 dots in the background. So at 6 you need 10 people for that dot and at 11 you need 15 people per dot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T12:26:25-07:00","id":1382806,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382790#post1382790\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Moving it to dice does two important things:<br /> <br /> First, the nerf is totally deserved as noted.</div> </div> </div> </div> like I said above, I don't feel one way or another about moving it to dice, though I agree that moving it to dice rather than automatic definitely keeps with the &quot;press your luck&quot; mechanic at play in the magick system. Having said that, even if it could cause you to botch, it's practically broken in a different way that you would get five additional dice <i>per roll</i> rather than just five successes at the end and that's it. If you have a Sanctum (so lots of your Vulgar stuff is going to coincidental) and you pack in your 25 people to sit around in the lotus position while you do <i>whatever</i>, it's not hard to engineer a situation in which you're casting some pretty powerful magick with +5 dice per roll (in addition whatever your Arete is of course), at diff 3. Yeah, those dice can botch, but when the difficulty is that low, the odds are super in your favor.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382790#post1382790\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The cap of 5? That I'm more hit or miss on. Though I do think that baakycalder is right that getting more and more people on the same page is hard enough there's clearly a diminishing returns issue. This is actually the book's position:<br /> <br /> &quot;It\u2019s possible to have a larger cult, of course \u2013 a popular<br /> evangelist might have hundreds of devotees. After a certain<br /> point, though, they can help you only so much.&quot;<br /> <br /> M20 p. 310</div> </div> </div> </div> I get that the book says that &quot;at some point there's diminishing returns&quot;, but that's completely at odds with basically everything else set forth in the rest of the materials. The overwhelming weight of consensual belief is the ultimate determining factor in <i>What Reality Is</i>, and the book (going back two editions and 20+ years) provides that believers supplement your ability to bend reality. But now they've tacked on, &quot;but only so much.&quot; Obviously the metaphysics of the game are whatever the book says they are--if the book said that the feathers of a snow owl each count as 1 dram of tass regardless of any other factors... that's what it would be. If the book were to say that the consensus has shifted enough now that teleportation is possible as long as you use a device that looks enough like a transporter from Star Trek... that's what it would be. But in neither case would it necessarily make it tonally or thematically consistent with the rest of the game as written, and as it's <i>been </i>written.<br /> <br /> To use the televangelist example, Joel Osteen's Lakewood Church in Houston gets about 43k congregants come through per week. Mind you, these are not necessarily like rock and roll fans going to a venue to see a show--these are people who explicitly believe in phenomena outside what is explicable by science, and they think Osteen's has a line up to the source. Even if all 47k wouldn't qualify as Acolytes (and really they shouldn't), what about the top 5% most fervent, dyed-in-the-wool, true believing, devout, unwavering of that following? What about the top 1%? And if modern examples don't move you, you can look at history to the men and women who ruled ancient empires while holding themselves out as literal gods. Say what you will, if you have the juice to undertake an architectural project like the pyramids with the technology available at the time, you probably have the juice to get a 500 to 1,000 (or more) people to stand around in abject, faithful devotion to your (or Osteen for that matter), praying that he accomplish his goal, for his good health, etc., is not otherwise participate in the ritual in some way, and <i>actually believe </i>in what they're doing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382790#post1382790\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Baakycalder is also right about the idea that having even more people impacts the local Reality Zone, again, this is already a bonus in the text:<br /> <br /> &quot;If you\u2019ve got over 100 people involved, certain vulgar<br /> Effects (summonings, gateways, raisings of the dead,<br /> and so forth) may be considered coincidental if there<br /> are no other witnesses around to contradict that impression.&quot;<br /> <br /> M20 p. 542.<br /> <br /> While it's not exactly changing the local Reality Zone permanently, getting a massive gathering going can turn vulgar w/o witnesses magic into coincidental. That's a huge deal in and of itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except... so what? If you can't benefit from more than 25 people helping you at a time (Cult 5), and you can get yourself a middling Sanctum (Sanctum 2-3) to fit those people into (even the nerfed M20 sanctums that are <i>uniformly</i> no larger than 500 sq. ft. regardless of level, will be plenty for many paradigms--particularly the sit-around-and-chant/pray/meditate sorts), most stuff you cast (i.e., the stuff isn't Always-Vulgar-No-Matter-What) is going to be coincidental anyway.<br /> <br /> The cap just seems like a nerf in search of a problem. The check on mages with huge cults has always been the sheer difficulty of putting that together, and then, <i>other</i> mages/factions with huge cults--including the Technocracy, who can, with probably little difficulty, have just about anyone branded a new &quot;Heaven's Gate&quot; or &quot;Branch Davidians&quot; (lol, the background <i>is </i>called &quot;Cult&quot; after all!) It's baffling to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":2319,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","user":"CaptOtter","date":". 04-20-2020, 03:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T12:32:04-07:00","id":1382807,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mercurial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382803#post1382803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would propose a compromise rule making it so that you have to add 5 people after every 5 dots in the background. So at 6 you need 10 people for that dot and at 11 you need 15 people per dot.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm just not using the rule at all. It's never been a problem in any game I've played in, run, or heard; and the <i>argumentum ad absurdum</i>, whereby someone with 100 or 1,000 followers gets 20 or 200 successes, is so difficult as to be practically impossible for a player to pull off in a regular chornicle (via so many perfectly reasonable stumbling blocks), unless the whole point of the chronicle is &quot;dueling massive cult leaders&quot;, in which case it's explicitly the point of the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T13:45:16-07:00","id":1382819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382806#post1382806\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I get that the book says that &quot;at some point there's diminishing returns&quot;, but that's completely at odds with basically everything else set forth in the rest of the materials.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a fairly hard to justify thing when talking about how the game models things with mechanics. The whole system is based around diminishing returns. Every dot of Attributes of Abilities you buy costs more, and gets you less statistically. Diminishing returns is so hard baked into the system pretty much any time you don't see it there's people that consider whatever the example is OP.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But in neither case would it necessarily make it tonally or thematically consistent with the rest of the game as written, and as it's <i>been </i>written. </div> </div> </div>OK. Please show me where, in any of the books newer or older, using acolyte groups in the hundreds ever happens? When it that part of the tone or theme of the game that this sort of thing is practical or functional given the difficulty of organization and finding enough acolytes at once? Just because the old rules never capped it doesn't mean the old books were written like you could get the numbers they allowed you to when describing the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Say what you will, if you have the juice to undertake an architectural project like the pyramids with the technology available at the time, you probably have the juice to get a 500 to 1,000 (or more) people to stand around in abject, faithful devotion to your (or Osteen for that matter), praying that he accomplish his goal, for his good health, etc., is not otherwise participate in the ritual in some way, and <i>actually believe </i>in what they're doing. </div> </div> </div>I'd say this sentiment is drastically at odds with how the books have ever described the difference between normal believers in a religion and characters that would count as acolytes mechanically.<br /> <br /> As you've said, the vast majority of Osteen's audience don't count. The same would be true of ancient rules claiming divine power (esp. considering the much smaller populations back in the day, gathering hundreds of thousands of people together would be a huge event that could only be done a few times a year).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Except... so what? </div> </div> </div>The ability to turn vulgar w/o witness spells coincidental without being tied to a specific place is pretty nice to me. Sanctums are great, but they're stuck as a single spot. You can't rove around the back country with your Sanctum, doing lots of vulgar magic as coincidental magic like you can with Cult. That's so what.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (even the nerfed M20 sanctums that are <i>uniformly</i> no larger than 500 sq. ft.... </div> </div> </div>Yeah.. I'd take the -2 ritual diff and the 5 Arcane over needing massive size to my Sanctum any day. Those new bonuses are way, way more potent than the size of the building. Anyway, Chantries have always been described as having multiple Sanctums within them. If you want more than 500 sq ft of Sanctum, you just need multiple mages working as a Chantry that have compatible practices and so on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The cap just seems like a nerf in search of a problem. </div> </div> </div>Or it's just reflecting how the setting has always actually been, but never mechanically enforced before.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The check on mages with huge cults has always been the sheer difficulty of putting that together,... </div> </div> </div>How is that not what the cap represents? More than +5 is just too hard to put together to be worth it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and then, <i>other</i> mages/factions with huge cults--including the Technocracy, who can, with probably little difficulty, have just about anyone branded a new &quot;Heaven's Gate&quot; or &quot;Branch Davidians&quot; (lol, the background <i>is </i>called &quot;Cult&quot; after all!) </div> </div> </div>The Technocracy doesn't really have much in the way of Cult as written (not that they don't have any). In 1e and 2e they relied on brainwashing too much for their massive base of mundane agents to count, and in Revised they lost touch with humanity's real Consensus as people lost faith in scientific exceptionalism.<br /> <br /> The Union brands non-Union mages with terms like that all the time anyway... so what to that?<br /> <br /> Also, in a game like Mage, the interplay of what the word cult used to mean, vs. how we use it now, is rather interesting and not really well served with this sort of off-handed appeal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's baffling to me. </div> </div> </div>It's honestly baffling to me anyone would actually care this much about there being a cap on this. IME so few people used this part of the rules, the cap is fairly meaningless, and M20 does more to highlight actually using one of the best boosts to your magic in the game that so many people ignore.<br /> <br /> You also seem to be wrapped up in a WoD that's nothing like what's described. Acolytes are supposed to be rare. Not as rare as mages, but the idea of having a hundred, or hundreds of Acolytes in one place is something that's always been described as happening in the oldest, largest, and most powerful Chantries of all time, or before the change to make the Umbra more hostile, Horizon Realms where generations of people have been raised in service to mages. And those aren't one mages personal cult, but groups working for dozens of different mages at any given time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T17:14:45-07:00","id":1382850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a fairly hard to justify thing when talking about how the game models things with mechanics. The whole system is based around diminishing returns. Every dot of Attributes of Abilities you buy costs more, and gets you less statistically. Diminishing returns is so hard baked into the system pretty much any time you don't see it there's people that consider whatever the example is OP.</div> </div> </div> </div> There's diminished returns for some things, but not others. For instance, within the &quot;Acting in concert&quot; rules, there's no limit to how many mages can participate as full contributors to an effect; Enlightened assistants (mages with less dots than necessary to cast the full effect) are capped. It's inconsistent without any underlying explanation other than <i>that's just how it is</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK. Please show me where, in any of the books newer or older, using acolyte groups in the hundreds ever happens? When it that part of the tone or theme of the game that this sort of thing is practical or functional given the difficulty of organization and finding enough acolytes at once? Just because the old rules never capped it doesn't mean the old books were written like you could get the numbers they allowed you to when describing the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Second addition provides:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Un-Awakened acolytes may assist their companions as well; the mystick adds one success for every five participants to his ritual. Coordinating such a large gatherings may take hours per roll, and such rituals should be played for maximum effect (Manipulation + Expression rolls are good for this.) Acolytes do not count as &quot;witnesses&quot; for vulgar magick under these circumstances. If over 100 were involved, some vulgar Effects might even be counter as coincidental, <i>if </i>no other witnesses were around to contradict them.<br /> <br /> Only one option may be used at a time.<br /> <br /> M:tAs (2nd Ed.), 172. </div> </div> </div> Revised provides as follows:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Un-Awakened acolytes may assist their compatriots as well. The main casting group adds one success for every five participants in this ritual. Coordinating such large gatherings may take hours per roll, and such rituals should be played out for maximum effect (which is also a good opportunity to use those Social Traits). Acolytes who believe enough to help in this fashion obviously do not count as &quot;witnesses&quot; in the vulgar sense. If over 100 people were involved, some vulgar Effects ought even be considered coincidental if no other witnesses were around to contradict them.<br /> <br /> M:tAs (Rev.), 154. </div> </div> </div> So here we have a couple of <i>almost </i>identical passages. You can't say they just copy/pasted, because it's clear that someone read the 2nd edition version of the passage and modified not just the text (the changes to which are obvious based on just a side-by-side comparison), but the mechanics as well. In Second Edition (as in M20) you're limited to choosing one of several different types of assistance--in Revised you're not. That's a system change. In Second edition, they specify Manipulation + Expression; in Revised you're simply admonished to make players flex their Social Traits; I understand that, as written, Second Edition was really just<i> suggesting </i>Manipulation + Expression rather than making an ironclad requirement--but the point is that the people who wrote Revised stopped to look at these rules, evaluated them, and made certain changes that were more than just word choice and writing style. In other words, there was some deliberation over mechanics here; this section was looked at with an eye towards whether system stuff was jibing with the rest of the product.<br /> <br /> Having said that, and despite the rewrites, in both books, you have a paragraph that provides a hard-and-fast rule (Every 5 Acolytes participating in a ritual provide one success), followed by qualifications and additions to the rule (i.e., how long it can take to accomplish this feat, what might be necessary to get people to agree to do it in the first place--logistical considerations basically), and then, in the same paragraph, the authors specifically provide an additional rule that only comes into effect when your followers hit the triple digits!<br /> <br /> So this is not my head cannon wank--that you got 1 success for every Acolytes, ergo, this must scale up and up and up! This is not some pet exploit I think I invented. The book <b>specifically contemplates</b> a situation in which you would get a <i>more </i>than 100 Acolytes together, and then it gives you an additional rule to apply in those circumstances--but just that one rule; no cap.<br /> <br /> So am I supposed to believe that when they sat down to deliberate over what the systematic effects of having 100+ Acolytes, it simply <i>did not occur </i>to the authors of Second Edition (who wrote the rule and paragraph from whole cloth), or the authors of Revised (who looked at the rule and paragraph closely enough to re-word much of it, and change some of the mechanics), that people would read this and go &quot;oh, you get 1 automatic success for ever 5 Acolutes--and you can get 100 or more together, which would give you 20 successes, but it's really really hard!&quot; Seriously, they wrote/rewrote a handful of specials rule that apply to having triple-digit Acolytes helping on a ritual, but did not throw in a rule to limit the effectiveness of those followers.<br /> <br /> You'll excuse me if I don't buy this was some casual oversight, and that all M20 is doing is putting into writing what was always meant to be, because it didn't occur to anyone that when they wrote that every 5 Acolytes gets you a success, and they immediately mention the speed bumps to getting 100+ of these people together, that people would not naturally do the math.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say this sentiment is drastically at odds with how the books have ever described the difference between normal believers in a religion and characters that would count as acolytes mechanically.<br /> <br /> As you've said, the vast majority of Osteen's audience don't count. The same would be true of ancient rules claiming divine power (esp. considering the much smaller populations back in the day, gathering hundreds of thousands of people together would be a huge event that could only be done a few times a year).</div> </div> </div> </div> That's why I said the top 1% of Osteen's followers. Even if you were to pick the top 0.5% (like the 99.5 percentile) of devout follower, it would still be 215 people. That ain't no slouch. And I will bet you dollars to doughnuts that the top .05% of Joel Osteen's followers would kill and die for that man--so absolute is their belief in everything he says and does. But that's the benefit to having a following in the tens of thousands.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The ability to turn vulgar w/o witness spells coincidental without being tied to a specific place is pretty nice to me. Sanctums are great, but they're stuck as a single spot. You can't rove around the back country with your Sanctum, doing lots of vulgar magic as coincidental magic like you can with Cult. That's so what.</div> </div> </div> </div> And you're roving around the back country with a 100 or more people? Because <i>that's</i> not conspicuous. I have no doubt that you can think of an effect that would be better suited to being done in the middle of a grassy meadow rather than in a temple or someone's mansion ballroom, or whatever. I can too. But the fact is that given the grandiose difficulty of assembling the hundred=plus people necessary to make something vulgar coincidental, you're probably just better doing it in your sanctum with a quarter of that number.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah.. I'd take the -2 ritual diff and the 5 Arcane over needing massive size to my Sanctum any day. Those new bonuses are way, way more potent than the size of the building.</div> </div> </div> </div> There are so many different potential sources for difficulty breaks on effects that it's difficult to get SUPER excited about another. And the balancing between Arcane and size was present in <i>Guide to the Traditions</i> -- granted there was no provision for buying both your Sanctum's Arcane <i>and </i>size, but it's probably not a stretch that people just allowed players to diversify the background. As it stands, you're already paying <i>double</i> for Sanctum in M20, so it's not like you even have the <i>option</i> of spending just one point on a closet in your house.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or it's just reflecting how the setting has always actually been, but never mechanically enforced before.</div> </div> </div> </div> See Above, where I dispute that this was the way it's always been. Again, it stretches the bounds of credulity to think that two different teams of people working years apart would have looked at the same information and rules, and not realized the implication of what they'd written--or even more bizarre--have assumed that people would just know that there's a cap on it, without them saying so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How is that not what the cap represents? More than +5 is just too hard to put together to be worth it.</div> </div> </div> </div> Clearly not &quot;too hard to put together&quot; if they specifically contemplate a situation in which you assemble 100+ people.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's honestly baffling to me anyone would actually care this much about there being a cap on this.</div> </div> </div> </div> Why does anyone care at all about any rule that they don't have to use if they don't want to? Why do we care enough to write these diatribes about a world of make-believe? Why do I want to understand the rationale here? I just <i>do</i>, and I don't think it was a case of &quot;this how everyone always thought this worked, but we're putting it down on paper now&quot; for the reasons I already put forward above multiple times.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">IME so few people used this part of the rules, the cap is fairly meaningless, and M20 does more to highlight actually using one of the best boosts to your magic in the game that so many people ignore.</div> </div> </div> </div> My player definitely never ignored the boost--can't speak for others. But I agree it would be incredibly difficult to abuse. The only purpose I thought it was served was to explain how certain things that would probably requires hundreds of successes would be even remotely achievable (e.g., creating Horizon).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382819#post1382819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You also seem to be wrapped up in a WoD that's nothing like what's described. Acolytes are supposed to be rare. Not as rare as mages, but the idea of having a hundred, or hundreds of Acolytes in one place is something that's always been described as happening in the oldest, largest, and most powerful Chantries of all time, or before the change to make the Umbra more hostile, Horizon Realms where generations of people have been raised in service to mages. And those aren't one mages personal cult, but groups working for dozens of different mages at any given time.</div> </div> </div> </div> You say this, and yet all three most recent editions of the game specifically provide a rule that applies to situations in which you get 100 such individuals together. If Acolytes are so rare, and the idea of getting 100 is so utterly beyond the capacity of the modern world, why would they not say that explicitly, rather than advise that it takes hours to coordinate the effort, and that they'll have certain expectations from the practitioner, and the rolls (or kinds of rolls) that would need to be performed in order to flex the social muscle necessary to execute this? It would be bizarre to posit that they provided so much explanation, writing, and rules to define a functionally impossible situation (this is not, after all, a historical supplement). At a minimum, the &quot;edition agnostic&quot; lip service paid by the game means that you could play a game during which neither, Horizon, Concordia, or Doissetep have fallen; is it really so implausible to posit that not a single one of the traditions would have been able to gather 100 Acolytes in Horizon to participate in a ritual? To bring it to the real world for a moment, over 900 people died at Jonestown when Jim Jones basically commanded that everyone &quot;drink the Kool Aid,&quot; knowing full well that it would kill them. But, maybe not all of those people might have assisted Jim Jones the mage in casting an effect, or maybe <i>really </i>believed to the extent necessary--after all, a handful of people were shot trying to flee (but are still considered part of the body count), and some people were children who didn't know what was happening. But if even 1 out of 9 people were a &quot;true believer&quot; who so fervently believed in Jones that they'd take their own life, I think don't know what basis we'd have to think those people's faith in Jones would not rise to the level necessary to trigger the systematic benefit.<br /> <br /> I'll leave you with this baffling piece of writing from M20: One page 532 they're describing what all the different kinds of Allies, Assistants, and Cults do. Every category of <i>thing</i> is separated out from every other category by a bullet point, and an alternating color background. There is a category here &quot;Assistants&quot; listed separate from &quot;Cult&quot;. The Assistants language reads as follows (emphasis mine):<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Assistants are trained Sleepers</b> who can handle mundane tasks, possibly holding down the fort with various self-powered Wonders. In Technocracy-speak, these are the extraordinary citizens; for the Council, they\u2019re acolytes. <b>Regardless of the terminology, these folks provide </b>backup, research, security, companionship, and <b>occasional help with Effects whose practices they understand</b>. <b>Rules-wise, these folks can provide a sympathetic crowd for your magickal Effects\u2026 which, in turn</b>, makes those Effects easier to cast, <b>provides extra successes for your roll</b>, and can even make certain vulgar Effects coincidental. </div> </div> </div>Honestly, I don't even know what to make of this.There's no background for &quot;Assistants&quot;; there's no ratio given; there's certainly no cap. It's almost like Assistants are what I once understood &quot;Acolytes&quot; to be, and a &quot;Cult&quot; is a wholly separate and additional category of <i>thing</i>. Certainly the Cults are not described as Acolytes in M20 (though they are under the &quot;Cult&quot; background in <i>Guide to the Traditions</i>; and the word Acolyte only appears like three times in all of M20. Honestly, my head is spinning at that point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T18:29:27-07:00","id":1382867,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382850#post1382850\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> There's diminished returns for some things, but not others.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, and the &quot;not others&quot; examples tend to be very questionable parts of the rules. There's no cap on mages helping, probably because mages are meant to be rare enough that having enough that are capable of acting in concert in the same place at the same time is hard enough it's never really come up, where things like Cult made the acolyte part more apparent in how potent it is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Second addition provides:<br /> Revised provides as follows: </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's no what I'm talking about. Cite actual descriptions of the setting. Show me all the Chantries with hordes of acolytes. The characters with massive follows of acolytes. Anything that describes acolytes existing en mass enough to have this be a significant impact on how the game is presented instead of how easy it is for people to use this rule to get a ton of successes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other words, there was some deliberation over mechanics here; this section was looked at with an eye towards whether system stuff was jibing with the rest of the product. </div> </div> </div>So.. how is M20 any different, and why is it getting you so worked up? Can't the same exact thing be said of M20?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...and then it gives you an additional rule to apply in those circumstances--but just that one rule; no cap. </div> </div> </div>So, serious question:<br /> <br /> What do you think the point of the social rules previous editions called for was? What happened if it failed? Did it get harder the larger the group?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You'll excuse me if I don't buy this was some casual oversight, and that all M20 is doing is putting into writing what was always meant to be,... </div> </div> </div>The authors of multiple WoD games have made far more unlikely explanations for rules changes.<br /> <br /> And the casual oversight here would be that people would actually make use of this frequently, to get lots of successes instead of the in-game difficulty and social rolls keeping things contained, so M20 decided to just give a hard cap instead of a soft cap of how well you can social roll people into getting together and working on your ritual.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I will bet you dollars to doughnuts that the top .05% of Joel Osteen's followers would kill and die for that man--so absolute is their belief in everything he says and does. But that's the benefit to having a following in the tens of thousands. </div> </div> </div>A cult of personality isn't the same thing as believing in Awakened magic. If people being hardcore members of a religion was enough, the Akashayana, Celestial Chorus, Chakravanti, and Ahl-i-Batin would be equals to the Technocracy in terms of Consensus considering the vast number of believers in the worlds largest religions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And you're roving around the back country with a 100 or more people? Because <i>that's</i> not conspicuous. </div> </div> </div>It's conspicuous now, sure. It used to be (and this is a fairly recent change) quite normal. That's sort of the the struggle of the Traditions: their methods rely on social norms that become outdated. A Chorister with a travelling tent revival, or a bunch of Cultists running a traveling circus is clearly something the Technocracy tried hard to kill off. That said, there's large enough groups living either way off the grid, in social isolation, or even nomadic life styles despite the pressures against such things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have no doubt that you can think of an effect that would be better suited to being done in the middle of a grassy meadow rather than in a temple or someone's mansion ballroom, or whatever. I can too. But the fact is that given the grandiose difficulty of assembling the hundred=plus people necessary to make something vulgar coincidental, you're probably just better doing it in your sanctum with a quarter of that number. </div> </div> </div>This is Mage. That whole &quot;Focus&quot; thing is supposed to matter a lot here. IME, mages are not nearly cookie cutter enough for everyone to prefer a Sanctum over a large following of people that will join them on the move.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Clearly not &quot;too hard to put together&quot; if they specifically contemplate a situation in which you assemble 100+ people. </div> </div> </div>When you snip out key words in my phrasing to change the meaning of what I said? You might as well just say, &quot;I'm really just pissed off they capped my way to power-game like an asshole and force everyone I play with to go down the same route to ultimate power, all my talk about anything else is bullshit.&quot;<br /> <br /> Because that's all you come off as trying to say.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why does anyone care at all about any rule that they don't have to use if they don't want to? </div> </div> </div>What does this have to do with anything?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why do I want to understand the rationale here? I just <i>do</i>,.... </div> </div> </div>Well, as I said, none of us are Brucato. If you're only going to be happy with his official response, go to Facebook.<br /> <br /> Otherwise, stop being a dick about people trying to give their interpretations of the rationale. You don't agree? Fine. You want to debate it even? OK. But your responses clearly show you don't really want to understand the rationale. You want to rant about a change to the game, otherwise you'd be working to refine an understanding rather than denouncing anything that might explain it because that's clearly not what you're acting like you want even if it's what you say you want.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only purpose I thought it was served was to explain how certain things that would probably requires hundreds of successes would be even remotely achievable (e.g., creating Horizon). </div> </div> </div>Again, then why are you so adamant about some perfect explanation for it if you agree it's basically an uber-rare thing that almost never actually comes up?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You say this, and yet all three most recent editions of the game specifically provide a rule that applies to situations in which you get 100 such individuals together. </div> </div> </div>Yeah. I say, &quot;it's possible to gather a lot of acolytes, it's just super rare and the setting is presented that way.&quot; Having a rule that applies to it doesn't contradict that, because I didn't say it was impossible to have 100+ acolytes in one place. Again, your &quot;reinterpretations&quot; of my posts don't paint you as wanting to really engage with anything other than people sharing in your ranting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'll leave you with this baffling piece of writing from M20: </div> </div> </div>Editing mistakes aren't baffling... they're just editing mistakes. Does every typo in the text cause you the same bafflement?<br /> <br /> You're clearly reaching and it shows. Your spinning around so much that you get dizzy, and blaming the book instead of yourself for getting dizzy.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-20T22:32:37-07:00","id":1382905,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, that's no what I'm talking about. Cite actual descriptions of the setting. Show me all the Chantries with hordes of acolytes. The characters with massive follows of acolytes. Anything that describes acolytes existing en mass enough to have this be a significant impact on how the game is presented instead of how easy it is for people to use this rule to get a ton of successes.</div> </div> </div> </div> Doissetep; Concordia (not a Chantry, but nevertheless); or for a historical example, Mistridge. There were 30,000 human beings in Horizon at any given time per the book <i>Horizon</i>, 15k of which were in Concordia--even accepting that the overwhelming majority of those people were just people going about their lives, you don't think any of the 9 traditions had enough Acolytes in the Realm at any given time to put together 100 of them for a ritual?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, serious question:<br /> <br /> What do you think the point of the social rules previous editions called for was? What happened if it failed? Did it get harder the larger the group?</div> </div> </div> </div> I thought--and still think--it's always been there to provide a plausible way for the ST to create a soft, but largely insurmountable check on getting enough people together to actually just chuck the rules. It's hard enough for players with 25-30 cultists/acolytes (i.e., Cult 5) to keep all those people happy--and from one game to the next they'll fail a roll during downtime, a handful of people will leave, and now they gotta take time to find new prospects, recruit them, indoctrinate them, etc. Doing this with 100 people would be insanely difficult--that's the check on the abuse you seem to think (per your last post) I so revel in.<br /> <br /> If it <i>fails</i> (by which I assume you mean the <i>soft </i>check fails to stop someone from accumulating these successes) you've just lost control of the table at that point. I approach the issue from two angles: the first angle, the in-real-world difficulty of getting 100 people to even sort of buy into heterodox, anti-scientific beliefs about supernatural phenomena, and <i>then</i>,getting those 100 people get along well enough to all participate in a ritual that would theoretically takes hours, and realistically, a commitment of one or more entire <i>days</i>. These considerations alone make this prohibitely time consuming and a pain in the ass, particularly if there's stuff actively going on in the story--and frankly, it's fair, because human beings are notoriously difficult to wrangle.<br /> <br /> The second angle is that, as the ST, you (or at least <i>I</i>) tune the difficulty of obtaining certain resources based on what the game itself shows says &quot;a lot&quot; of a given thing is. Even prior to M20, the game's actual stat for Cult only went up to 5--I didn't take this to be a hard cap precisely for the reasons I've already explained here and in prior posts, and because I understood certain BGs to top out at 5 because you just weren't supposed to let people start with more than that despite the fact that we could probably name people in the world with more than whatever the rating for &quot;5&quot; gave you for that background--Resources and Contacts come to mind. Obviously the first time I ever read WW main book, I understood that there must be people with Resources 6 or 7 or more because I could name people in the real world, at that moment, who had more than the top-end Res 5 amount of $5 million. But it also told me that that's what the game thought the highest amount of Resources a player could have and still be manageable in a regular chronicle. To bring this back to Acolytes and the Cult background -- the fact that it tops out of at 5 tells me that I need to tune to 5 being the top, and that anything higher than should be the subject of an entire story or something; or the sort of thing an entire tradition, or incredibly old Master or Archmage does.<br /> <br /> In other words, I see the whole &quot;getting 100+ Acolytes&quot; together to get a bunch of successes <i>thing </i>as more or less akin to having $1 billion, or being the Director of Central Intelligence. Sure it's <i><u>technically</u></i> possible (inasmuch as those things exist in real life), but good luck getting either one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And the casual oversight here would be that people would actually make use of this frequently, to get lots of successes instead of the in-game difficulty and social rolls keeping things contained, so M20 decided to just give a hard cap instead of a soft cap of how well you can social roll people into getting together and working on your ritual.</div> </div> </div> </div> I was saying that I didn't think the people wrote and put into print either version of that paragraph had just not realized that they had raised a strong inference that the mechanical benefits for Un-Awakened participants scaled very high.<br /> <br /> Having said that--I could see player abuse being a reason to re-write the power; I'd just never seen this abused, or seen anyone complain about the rule being abused (and thought the check to abuse was pretty obvious and easy on its face--see above). It would be like if in V20 they had decided to curtail the power of some super level 8 Thaumaturgical ritual... like... what prompted <i>that</i>?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A cult of personality isn't the same thing as believing in Awakened magic. If people being hardcore members of a religion was enough, the Akashayana, Celestial Chorus, Chakravanti, and Ahl-i-Batin would be equals to the Technocracy in terms of Consensus considering the vast number of believers in the worlds largest religions.</div> </div> </div> </div> The issue here is that despite being associated loosely with some religions, none of those Traditions's (or Crafts' for that matter, since we're looking at the Batini as well) truly align with a mortal religion. The Celestial Chorus aren't Christian, or Jewish, or Muslim -- despite some iconographic trappings that make them look Christian-ish, they're ultimately just devoutly monotheistic, and that's it. The Akashics aren't all Buddhist, and their beliefs and paradigm don't track perfectly with Buddhism (or Hinduism, Shinto, Jainism, etc.); the Chakravanti aren't even all necessarily Indian--some of them are Greek, and have all kinds of Greek &quot;flavoring&quot; to their Paradigm, so, I will submit to you that if your paradigm is such that esoteric ancient Greek stuff works as well as the stuff the Chakravanti are more commonly known for... you're probably not going to align well enough with the host culture or religions to just be able to piggy back on their services. And the Batini don't so much believe in Islam as they claim to have basically invented it, and it got out of control (which is maybe the most offensive thing I've ever read in a WW book--that this group of mages invented Islam; and I've read Gypsies). However, those religions do present a good starting point.<br /> <br /> I keep bringing up Joel Osteen because when you you start with that many congregants, you've gotten enough people in the general ballpark of belief and practice that you ultimately need that it now becomes about the process of singling out the die hards prepared to believe in what the other tens of thousands are believing... and maybe a little more. The conspiracy-minded ready to believe in deeper mysteries, the Cluster-A people with a particular penchant for magical thinking. To get to 100 good candidates, you don't even need a whole number percentage of the 43k; 0.25% (a quarter of a percent), gets you there. Would it take time and so forth? Sure, those people are there, and ready to believe in something more.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When you snip out key words in my phrasing to change the meaning of what I said?</div> </div> </div> </div> I apologize about this--I really do. It certainly wasn't intentional; I probably just genuinely fixated on something you said and lost something in the shuffle. Having said that, I said, and <i>you </i>quoted:<br /> <br /> <b>The check on mages with huge cults has always been the sheer difficulty of putting that together,... </b><br /> <br /> And your complete response to that snippet from me was: <i>How is that not what the cap represents? More than +5 is just too hard to put together to be worth it.</i><br /> <br /> My reply to your complete statement is, first, the cap of 5 appears to me to be a hard cap set by reality--and judging by your other statements above, I think you think that too, so I don't know what you were trying to say here. I also think that it should be very difficult for players to get the bigger cults, but it should still be achievable by <i>someone</i> (NPC plot device what have you).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You might as well just say, &quot;I'm really just pissed off they capped my way to power-game like an asshole and force everyone I play with to go down the same route to ultimate power, all my talk about anything else is bullshit.&quot;<br /> <br /> Because that's all you come off as trying to say.</div> </div> </div> </div> You're clearly reading some kind of hostile animus in my responses; I'm polemical, and I have my position, but I haven't been unkind, or insulted you; I'm not moving the goal posts, and I'm not trolling. I think you and I have gotten along enough elsewhere in the forums in the past that you know I'm not generally one to get personal about shit; I'm also not one to back down if I think one thing or another very strongly, but I'm not insulting people's intelligence or anything (though I may take shots at people's taste in the occassional V5 thread---so sue me). But, regardless of anything else, I genuinely and truly apologize if I got too fiery somewhere and my affect was that of coming at you in a wrong way; hasn't been my intention.<br /> <br /> With regards to addressing the substance of what your barb--I said at the top, and throughout, that the ability to cheese magick by getting gobs of Cultists/Acolytes/whatever is the justification for huge/overarching, 10k foot view plot machinations in my chronicle; so regardless of anything else, I more or less have to keep it. It's not something any PC in my game has done or can do, and it's not something I expect to be able to do when I play; point of fact, I've never played a character with a cult or any appreciable number of acolytes--like one or two retainers at most because I was too used to the in-game convenience of playing a vampire with ghouls to do menial shit for my character.<br /> <br /> Having said that, there might be something to be said for a game concept where the players play the Mage equivalent of Elders playing the jyhad, and each has a huge cult, but they have to try to outmaneuver one another, take pot shots at each others' cults, etc. Like <i>The Righteous Gemstones </i>meets <i>Game of Thrones</i>. But then, the whole point of that chronicle would be that this is what everyone is doing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What does this have to do with anything?</div> </div> </div> </div> If you're going to ask me why I care so much about this, my response is going to be to point out that I have no good reason to care about this any more than any of us ever have any good reason to care about any rule in any game. It creates an incongruity in the internal consistency of the game for me, and so I have a bug up my ass about it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, as I said, none of us are Brucato. If you're only going to be happy with his official response, go to Facebook.</div> </div> </div> </div> It's not that I'll <i>only </i>be happy with his official response, so much as the explanations you've offered are things I considered that don't make sense to me. The title of this entire thread is &quot;I don't understand why&quot; etc. etc. And it's true, I <i>don't</i> understand why. It's not difficult to come up with possible explanations for rule/mythos changes that (a) it was discordant with the game as a whole (either as it is or <i>should </i>be); (b) it was being abused; or (c) it was always meant to be whatever it is now, and they finally got around to clarifying. As I've explained, none of that is persuasive to me. You've given me your explanations as to why you think that, actually, all three potentially fit. I have explained why I'm not persuaded.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Otherwise, stop being a dick about people trying to give their interpretations of the rationale.</div> </div> </div> </div> Again, I'm sorry that I've come off like a dick.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You don't agree? Fine. You want to debate it even? OK.</div> </div> </div> </div> Like I said above, I started this thread having considered the usual reasons you change something, and unpersuaded it's any of those--or at least none of those made sense. I appreciate your time and your words on the matter, but just because you generously took the time to write it all out, doesn't mean I'm obligated to concede the point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But your responses clearly show you don't really want to understand the rationale.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think I understand what you're saying.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You want to rant about a change to the game, otherwise you'd be working to refine an understanding rather than denouncing anything that might explain it because that's clearly not what you're acting like you want even if it's what you say you want.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think this is uncharitable to me. So far there's nothing you've said that I went 'oh shit, I hadn't seen that part of the rule,' or , 'I clearly misread this that or the other'. You and I are looking at the same texts--basically all the same evidence--and you've reached and proposed conclusions that don't resolve the incongruity that I see here. Just because what moves the needle for you doesn't move it for me doesn't mean that I just didn't consider what you were saying in the first place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, then why are you so adamant about some perfect explanation for it if you agree it's basically an uber-rare thing that almost never actually comes up?</div> </div> </div> </div> It makes the game incongruous to me, and worse, feels arbitrary. And that bothers the hell out of me. I mean, I guess at some point I gotta just go &quot;it is what it is&quot;; but that will be supremely unsatisfying. I'm not exactly sure what I was expecting for someone to tell me that would make me go &quot;ah, okay, I see what they did that now&quot;; maybe someone would point out some poorly placed bit of text in a prior edition that made it clear I'd been wrong all these years. Again, not sure what I was expecting; but these forums (or some version of them) have surprised me <i>many </i>times before.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah. I say, &quot;it's possible to gather a lot of acolytes, it's just super rare and the setting is presented that way.&quot; Having a rule that applies to it doesn't contradict that, because I didn't say it was impossible to have 100+ acolytes in one place. Again, your &quot;reinterpretations&quot; of my posts don't paint you as wanting to really engage with anything other than people sharing in your ranting.</div> </div> </div> </div> What I'm saying is that it being super rare and difficult to get 100 Acolytes together should not be represented--mechanically--by capping the raw dice benefit of actually pulling off the ridiculous feat. It's like and 8th vampire diablerizing a 4th gen vampire--no one should rely on the idea that that's readily doable; and as far as players are concerned, it should be impossible outside of a ST hand-wave. But you still wouldn't (or shouldn't) change the system effects (good and bad) of actually accomplishing that.<br /> <br /> That's what <i>I'm</i> saying. If it's not responsive to what <i>you're </i>saying, then maybe I <i>don't </i>understand what your construction of the rules is--I thought you were saying that the diminishing magical returns on getting a lot of people together (as now represented by the hard cap of 5 dice/formerly successes) has always been the rule, and that the setting in prior editions bears that out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Editing mistakes aren't baffling... they're just editing mistakes. Does every typo in the text cause you the same bafflement?</div> </div> </div> </div> I understand mistakes in editing, but the point in my describing in excrutiating detail where and how the text is presented was to illustrate that there is something very deliberate-seeming about this. It's not just a word out of place, a transposed letter, or anything else that would make it look like a scrivener's error. I could assume that they left that entire section in by mistake, but it's a slippery slope to assume that anything that doesn't jibe with one's understanding of the text <i>must </i>be there in error. Regardless of whether I like the conclusion, I think the best practice is to try to give as much of the text as much meaning and effect as possible when I'm formulating my understanding of it. Having said that--sometimes some shit is only explicable as an error. So do you think the entire paragraph (and the heading for the section saying &quot;Assistants&quot;) was left in by mistake, or is there some other way you would read this to make it work with the rest of the book?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're clearly reaching and it shows.</div> </div> </div> </div> Reaching for what exactly? What is the the brass ring for me here, as you see it? There's no debate as to how M20 functions or how it should be applied--25, 50, or a 500 Acolytes (whatever number), you're capped at 5 dice. I don't <i>think </i>we're arguing over whether this has always been the rule, because I don't see us as having disagreed that this is the first instance of this language in any edition. And It's not like I <i>need</i> M20 to function one way or another for any practical purpose, because I can (and do) just ignore the rule. Put another way: I don't need to reach for anything. There really isn't anything at stake here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382867#post1382867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your spinning around so much that you get dizzy, and blaming the book instead of yourself for getting dizzy.</div> </div> </div> </div> Honestly, I think at some point you need to stop and evaluate the degree to which your tone has contributed to the unfriendliness that this thread has taken on, because this last post of yours has been incredibly hostile. I'll concede and apologize for being short, accidentally misquoting you (or at least not having taken the care to appreciate the context of something I quoted you as saying), and being dogged about where I'm at with this--but you've insulted and questioned my motives (again, as if there were real stakes here), you've basically called me disingenuous, quite frankly, you've gotten downright nasty. I'm not gonna say &quot;I'm hurt&quot;, because I don't know you--not <i>really</i>. But if shit is going to get this heated over this, I'll just stop, promise to be better about quoting you moving forward, and hope that we can continue being friendly to one another in the future.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":2319,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","user":"CaptOtter","date":". 04-21-2020, 01:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-21T02:18:23-07:00","id":1382932,"title":"","content":"Personally I loved the Acolyte cult system, and I ported it somewhat to other games.<br /> <br /> I feel dramatically if you can pull off a thousands of Cultist super festival/sacrifice in your hidden temple or commune then sure have a bonus above the 5, or maybe exponential amounts of Cultists start turning the dice to successes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-21T05:09:53-07:00","id":1382957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382905#post1382905\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Doissetep;</div> </div> </div> </div>The largest, oldest extant (before it wasn't), and most powerful Tradition Chantry was detailed as having just 400 servants (not all of whom are acolytes by the text), spread out among at least 17 earthly nodes and the Shard Realm itself (the majority in the Realm). Serving ten cabals of some of the most powerful mages ever (many of them centuries old). Considering the politicking and backstabbing going on, the idea of any one Cabal, let alone any one mage, of Doissetep getting 100 acolytes together out of that pool for a ritual is fairly unrealistic.<br /> <br /> Doissetep actually highlights my point really well: the Chantry that's supposed to set the bar for ridiculously powerful, would rarely see a ritual where you'd get more that +5 from acolytes, or the extra bonus for 100 of them (which wouldn't matter anyway). The setting simply isn't presented where mages have access to lots of acolytes. Lets say the average mage per cabal is 4 (the ones detailed range from 3 to 10, but 4 is the most common). That's 40 mages. Let's say 1/5 of the servants are unavailable for rituals tending to all the Nodes, and 9/10 of them are acolytes instead of consors of whatever stripe. That leaves just 288 acolytes to be split between 40 mages. That's seven acolytes per mage, and less than 30 per cabal. Doissetep, on an average day, was basically already operating at a level where the +5 cap wouldn't change anything. And that's not even trying to account for it being a mutli-Tradition Chantry despite being dominated by the Hermetics, meaning there's no guarantee sharing of acolytes is that &quot;simple.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is what the setting presents at the high mark that most mages will only get to visit. Which is to say, the setting isn't presented where there's a lot of acolytes running around. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Concordia (not a Chantry, but nevertheless); or for a historical example, Mistridge. There were 30,000 human beings in Horizon at any given time per the book <i>Horizon</i>, 15k of which were in Concordia--even accepting that the overwhelming majority of those people were just people going about their lives, you don't think any of the 9 traditions had enough Acolytes in the Realm at any given time to put together 100 of them for a ritual? </div> </div> </div>Pulling hundred people out of their daily lives, even with a population that large, is highly disruptive. So, no, not at &quot;any given time,&quot; that's something that takes planning, or infrastructure built specifically for it. Could they do it? Sure. But this is also the most extreme example and only exists in the Umbra. Considering Horizon's 150 mages to 30K humans compared to Doissetep's ~40 mages to 400 humans, this is clearly the outlier of outliers in the setting (and it's supposed to be).<br /> <br /> This is a setting where 200 of the most powerful mages to ever exist still have to pull strings to get 100 acolytes together at one time. That's how the game presents itself. The huge majority of mages out there stuck with a Chantry that only exists on Earth with a single node aren't going to have access to acolytes in number unless it's a constant and pressing effort for them. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I thought--and still think--it's always been there to provide a plausible way for the ST to create a soft, but largely insurmountable check on getting enough people together to actually just chuck the rules. </div> </div> </div>So, what's the big logical or mental leap between &quot;soft but largely insurmountable&quot; and &quot;hard,&quot; that's not clicking? Is it really that hard to buy, &quot;we didn't like a soft rule that made something look possible even if it's really meant to be nigh impossible, so we made a hard rule so people can just get back to the game,&quot; as a reason for the change?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If it <i>fails</i> (by which I assume you mean the <i>soft </i>check fails to stop someone from accumulating these successes) you've just lost control of the table at that point. </div> </div> </div>Right. So this, to me, just seems like they streamlined it as part of making it dice instead of successes. Bonus dice usually help, but might screw you up, and now you don't need a second roll to get the same possible results. I'm not talking just about 100 people here, since the soft check was never just on 100 people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But it also told me that that's what the game thought the highest amount of Resources a player could have and still be manageable in a regular chronicle. </div> </div> </div>See, this is pretty key, but at odds with other things you said (like what a starting character's cap is). If you're comfortable with the idea of 5s in Backgrounds being the game communicating what's manageable in a regular chronicle... than the new cap is just doing the same thing. This is part of why this conversation has me frustrated. You say things like this, but it's really not clear why this thinking doesn't cover the rule in question. &quot;+5 is what we thing is the highest amount of Cult bonus a player could have and still be manageable in a regular chronicle.&quot; What's insufficient about this applied to the new rule?<br /> <br /> Because this isn't the same as &quot;well 5 is just the normal cap unless you do something big in the story.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I was saying that I didn't think the people wrote and put into print either version of that paragraph had just not realized that they had raised a strong inference that the mechanical benefits for Un-Awakened participants scaled very high. </div> </div> </div>This isn't the assumption I'm making though. The people that wrote that paragraph (keeping in mind Brucato was the lead designer on 2e and M20, so he was in charge of how both of those editions present this rule) could have very easily not realized that the soft cap they put in place via social rolls wasn't the strong cap needed to balance out the scaling potential of lots of acolytes helping out. When you consider things like magic boosting your Social Attributes, dropping your difficulty, and things like Merits that can add more dice/lower difficulty, that soft cap check doesn't hold up as well with lots of different systems and secondary/supplemental rules coming into play. A hard cap is a simple solution.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It would be like if in V20 they had decided to curtail the power of some super level 8 Thaumaturgical ritual... like... what prompted <i>that</i>? </div> </div> </div>Thaumaturgy and its rituals are capped at 5 even for Elders.<br /> <br /> This is also a poor comparison because there's a huge IC hurdle to getting 6+ powers in VtM that doesn't exist in Mage via Generation requirements.<br /> <br /> On top of that, the elder Disciplines are an absolute mess of power levels. Most VtM fans, if they saw just one elder Discipline get worked on, would be less confused by it happening, and more by only one of them getting futzed with when the vast majority of them need fixing. It's not like this is the only rules change in M20.<br /> <br /> Of course, something a bit closer in terms of player and/or character access and fan reaction's would be, say, changing some Disciplines while ignoring others... which is exactly what happened in V20 and the general response was, &quot;well at least Celerity is less broken and Potence grappling is more expensive, sucks that Fortitude is still both necessary and sucky at the same time.&quot;<br /> <br /> Or when W20 was in the works, the developers asked the community for suggestions on rules to revisit. I could not start to justify why some got addressed and others didn't, but they did because the developers were going to put some work into tweaking and fixing the rules; even if they didn't cover every last thing, or have the priorities I would have had. But getting past that impossible to penetrate decision making process, the individual rules that got changed got changed and it wasn't something that felt unprompted... all the 20th books have rules changes in them (generally small ones to band-aid various little things).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The issue here is that despite being associated loosely with some religions, none of those Traditions's (or Crafts' for that matter, since we're looking at the Batini as well) truly align with a mortal religion. </div> </div> </div>The Technocracy's philosophy doesn't truly align with any mortal one either.<br /> <br /> The issue is that over half of humans on Earth are members of a monotheistic religion. The most faithful 1% of them is still at least one, if not three orders of magnitude more people than all the active scientists on Earth without cutting their numbers for level of belief in the scientific method. Big numbers alone can't really be used like you're using them for Mage without the setting falling into incoherence. If half the world believes in there being one creator deity, and even generally agree on the attributes of that deity (since Christianity and Islam don't actually disagree much about what the divine is as much as what the message on how to act is), than that would either have to be part of the general Consensus Reality Zone, or it's way more complicated than that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I keep bringing up Joel Osteen because when you you start with that many congregants, </div> </div> </div>And combined with my previous point, and previous posts about the differences between faith and a cult of personality, this doesn't make the point you want it to make. There's 330 million US citizens, that doesn't mean there are acolytes of US democracy around that would quality for these bonuses if you tried to use magic to enforce US democratic norms. Even if 100 people out there are super die-hard do anything and everything for it, that doesn't mean it makes magic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My reply to your complete statement is, first, the cap of 5 appears to me to be a hard cap set by reality--and judging by your other statements above, I think you think that too, so I don't know what you were trying to say here. </div> </div> </div>I do not think that.<br /> <br /> I don't treat the rules as the reality of the game, I treat the rules as an approximation of the reality of the game. +5 isn't some new universal truth to the setting, it's just a different abstraction model of the setting. Whether a soft cap or a hard cap, the game is saying in the setting organizing too many people gets so hard the effort isn't worth it. It's just been phrased differently in moving from soft to hard..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It creates an incongruity in the internal consistency of the game for me, and so I have a bug up my ass about it. </div> </div> </div>And it feels, to me, like you're letting that bug get to you more than it seems like you know you should.<br /> <br /> We get bugs up our asses, it happens. But sometimes the right move is for other people to say, &quot;dude, you've just got a bug up your ass about this, and it seems like just letting it go is the only option because once you do all this is going to fall away.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have explained why I'm not persuaded. </div> </div> </div>And I've expressed that your explanations ring extremely hollow. It seriously feels like your attempts to explain why you're not persuaded are more justifications to not have to deal with them being persuasive positions. I'm not sure how you'd like me to go from there because all I can do is call that out if that's how I see it. I might have gotten exceedingly pissy about it last post, but the general point is one I still feel.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But you still wouldn't (or shouldn't) change the system effects (good and bad) of actually accomplishing that. </div> </div> </div>But, if I'm changing the mechanics of how to accomplish something, there's knock on effects to that. Moving from an extra roll to get a set number of bonus successes to a dice bonus causes more than enough mechanical disruption to justify changing how the caps on the bonus works too.<br /> <br /> The system effects are still &quot;potentially get lots of bonus successes, and maybe turn something vulgar w/o witness coincidental.&quot; the potential amount gained might be altered slightly, or just less predictable, but the actual system effects are the same thing.<br /> <br /> If I changed the Diablerie rule in how you achieve it, it could easily lead to a change in determining how much you get out of it. If I make it harder to pull off, making it easier to get the maximum bonuses would be a good thing to avoid nerfing it, and if I make it easier to do, making the bonuses less likely to be the best possible results would be good to avoid it becoming OP.<br /> <br /> The core idea isn't changing here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I thought you were saying that the diminishing magical returns on getting a lot of people together (as now represented by the hard cap of 5 dice/formerly successes) has always been the rule, and that the setting in prior editions bears that out. </div> </div> </div>I was saying that this was always the intent of the rules, as born out by prior editions, even if not actualized by them by using a soft cap that could be subverted for anyone invested in using Cult to the maximum extent possible instead of a hard one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So do you think the entire paragraph (and the heading for the section saying &quot;Assistants&quot;) was left in by mistake, or is there some other way you would read this to make it work with the rest of the book? </div> </div> </div>I think there was a simple editing error were the Assistant writer used the older rules of adding successes and it didn't get changed to adding dice. That change alone makes it work with the rest of the book just fine.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Reaching for what exactly? </div> </div> </div>Confirmation bias towards your feelings of incongruity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Honestly, I think at some point you need to stop and evaluate the degree to which your tone has contributed to the unfriendliness that this thread has taken on, because this last post of yours has been incredibly hostile. </div> </div> </div>Conceded, and hopefully improved on in your estimation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-21T09:51:16-07:00","id":1382988,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pulling hundred people out of their daily lives, even with a population that large, is highly disruptive. So, no, not at &quot;any given time,&quot; that's something that takes planning, or infrastructure built specifically for it. Could they do it? Sure. But this is also the most extreme example and only exists in the Umbra. Considering Horizon's 150 mages to 30K humans compared to Doissetep's ~40 mages to 400 humans, this is clearly the outlier of outliers in the setting (and it's supposed to be).</div> </div> </div> </div> I can concede that none of the Council of 9 is likely to be able to just buzz an assistant to ask that whatever number of Acolytes (for their paradigm obviously) be assembled and ready to assist with <i>whatever</i> any reliably small amount of time later. For my purposes, however, that &quot;flex&quot; is there; it's a card in their hand that they can play, should it be necessary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a setting where 200 of the most powerful mages to ever exist still have to pull strings to get 100 acolytes together at one time. That's how the game presents itself. The huge majority of mages out there stuck with a Chantry that only exists on Earth with a single node aren't going to have access to acolytes in number unless it's a constant and pressing effort for them.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think I've more than demonstrated that I agree that this is not an easy enough feat that your rank-and-file mage (i.e., PCs) have this as an option.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, what's the big logical or mental leap between &quot;soft but largely insurmountable&quot; and &quot;hard,&quot; that's not clicking? Is it really that hard to buy, &quot;we didn't like a soft rule that made something look possible even if it's really meant to be nigh impossible, so we made a hard rule so people can just get back to the game,&quot; as a reason for the change?</div> </div> </div> </div> If I say, &quot;I don't know why they did X,&quot; and you offer as a possible explanation, &quot;to fix Problem Y,&quot; the degree to which it's either easy or hard for me to <i>buy </i>that explanation is going to be tied to the degree to which I saw or conceived of Problem Y as actually having been <i>a problem</i> in the first place. I don't think you're telling me that this has been an issue at your table; it certainly hasn't been one at mine, and I don't recall ever seeing anyone bemoaning this (plenty of other things, but not this). In other words, as I presently understand the &quot;meta&quot; of this particular WoD game, this is not one of the systems or rules that get abused. Is it possible that my understanding is flaws, and that Brucato or one or more people on the M20 team have had an issue with this? Of course; and it the absence of any more persuasive, this will absolutely hang in my mind as one of a handful of possible scenarios that mustbe true in order to explain the change. But it's not like I needed anyone to tell me that a possible reason for the rule change was to stop the rule from being abused, so much as I didn't give it much consideration because in my experience and to my knowledge, the abuse simply wasn't there to begin with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right. So this, to me, just seems like they streamlined it as part of making it dice instead of successes. Bonus dice usually help, but might screw you up, and now you don't need a second roll to get the same possible results. I'm not talking just about 100 people here, since the soft check was never just on 100 people.</div> </div> </div> </div> The changing from successes to dice makes plenty of sense to me. From an editing/writing standpoint it makes sense because miswording a couple of paragraphs by a single word is imminently possible, and it's possible that everyone else who put eyes on the mistake just assumed that the person who wrote it did it on purpose; it makes sense to me from a game systems standpoint because making them dice increases tension in the exact way the magick system is supposed to be tense. What's stuck in my craw is the hard cap.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See, this is pretty key, but at odds with other things you said (like what a starting character's cap is). If you're comfortable with the idea of 5s in Backgrounds being the game communicating what's manageable in a regular chronicle... than the new cap is just doing the same thing. This is part of why this conversation has me frustrated. You say things like this, but it's really not clear why this thinking doesn't cover the rule in question. &quot;+5 is what we thing is the highest amount of Cult bonus a player could have and still be manageable in a regular chronicle.&quot; What's insufficient about this applied to the new rule?</div> </div> </div> </div> Because my issue is not, and has never been, that I thought this was realistically available to players and now it's not; it's a plot device mechanic--the place where I use the rule is to justify hand-wave-y things that far more powerful mages, or the Technocracy do. PC Question: &quot;How did they do XYZ&quot;; My Answer: &quot;The Technocracy had the juice to assemble enough Extraordinary Citizens to erect this crazy ward very quickly/crank out whatever charm/build this huge Voidship[,]&quot; or how the <i>Book of Chantries</i> doesn't actually give a system for making a realm; they'll give you a system for opening a node, and say could take as many as 20 or more successes--but the book won't even touch giving a hard number for realm-building, saying simply that it's hard enough that it could be the subject of it's own story or whatever, and that it's something that very high level characters do.I guess it could just be a narrative hand-wave that has no practical mechanical explanation within the rules as written (they're archmages/super masters/etc. -- they're <i>very mysterious oooooooo</i>), that's fine--but that kind of &quot;just because&quot; answer bothers me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This isn't the assumption I'm making though. The people that wrote that paragraph (keeping in mind Brucato was the lead designer on 2e and M20, so he was in charge of how both of those editions present this rule) could have very easily not realized that the soft cap they put in place via social rolls wasn't the strong cap needed to balance out the scaling potential of lots of acolytes helping out. When you consider things like magic boosting your Social Attributes, dropping your difficulty, and things like Merits that can add more dice/lower difficulty, that soft cap check doesn't hold up as well with lots of different systems and secondary/supplemental rules coming into play. A hard cap is a simple solution.</div> </div> </div> </div> Like I said elsewhere, in the absence of a better explanation, this is just going to be one of the things I hold up in my mind as likely being what the case is. Obviously if I can't work forward from the facts, as I understand them, to come up with a logical explanation for a rational person's actions, then I either have to conclude that (a) the person <i>isn't</i> rational (which would be an unwarranted and arrogant insult to Brucato and the other authors) <b>or </b>(b) that I simply have the facts wrong. But even then, actions taken based on a state of affairs or phenomenon that run totally contrary to my experiences, or what I understand other similarly situated people's experiences to be, is necessarily going to seem <i>weird </i>to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thaumaturgy and its rituals are capped at 5 even for Elders.</div> </div> </div> </div> Paths are capped at 5; there are handful of higher level Thaum rituals of varying degrees of utility. I mean, I get some of the existing for &quot;Big Plot Device&quot; reasons (like what I use un-capped Acolyte participation on rituals for)--but little or nothing that I think anyone would call a &quot;win button&quot;. Source: <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Thaumaturgy_Rituals\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Li...aturgy_Rituals</a> (the list is cited to the books where the rituals can be found).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is also a poor comparison because there's a huge IC hurdle to getting 6+ powers in VtM that doesn't exist in Mage via Generation requirements.</div> </div> </div> </div> I liken them to one another because they are, for me (in my mind), both technically achievable and doable things--but very very difficult, and not something you should just rely on being able to do at any given moment. Having said that, I otherwise generally agree with your other sentiments about V:tM/V20.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or when W20 was in the works, the developers asked the community for suggestions on rules to revisit. I could not start to justify why some got addressed and others didn't, but they did because the developers were going to put some work into tweaking and fixing the rules; even if they didn't cover every last thing, or have the priorities I would have had. But getting past that impossible to penetrate decision making process, the individual rules that got changed got changed and it wasn't something that felt unprompted... all the 20th books have rules changes in them (generally small ones to band-aid various little things).</div> </div> </div> </div> As an aside, I'd be interested to know what handful of things didn't get patched that you thought needed patching. For me, W20 is probably the best of the 20th anniversary games. I also happen to think the sun shines out of Bill Bridges' ass. So you know...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Technocracy's philosophy doesn't truly align with any mortal one either.</div> </div> </div> </div> Agreed, but, I think that being on the currently (or definitely--depending on your metaplot usage) winning side of the Ascension War means having an easier time recruiting than the guys with wands and pointy hats. But when I see pictures of NASA mission control, with dozens and dozens of people all working harmoniously and in sync with another towards a well-defined, singular immediate goal or purpose, what occurs to me (after common sentiments like 'wow, they put a man on the moon' and generally being impressed) is &quot;that's what big Technocratic rituals look like&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or when you see videos of the large hadron collider at CERN. Honestly, that high-level particle physics stuff may as well be Technocratic magick to me: I don't understand the workings, and only understand the purpose or product if it's dumbed down and explained in conclusory ways; I have no way to vet whether what I'm told is legit or just technobabble fast-talk--I just have faith that these are smart people who went to good schools, and understand something about the operation and functioning of the natural world that I do not, and that they can do things with this huge machine that will allow them to learn enough other information about the natural world and reality, that other such persons will eventually be able to engineer magnificent things that would look like inexplicable and magical to me right now. Ironically of course, it's those super big-brain people that might be the biggest issue for technocrats, since they have a very good understanding of what is and isn't actually scientifically achievable right now.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The issue is that over half of humans on Earth are members of a monotheistic religion. The most faithful 1% of them is still at least one, if not three orders of magnitude more people than all the active scientists on Earth without cutting their numbers for level of belief in the scientific method. Big numbers alone can't really be used like you're using them for Mage without the setting falling into incoherence. If half the world believes in there being one creator deity, and even generally agree on the attributes of that deity (since Christianity and Islam don't actually disagree much about what the divine is as much as what the message on how to act is), than that would either have to be part of the general Consensus Reality Zone, or it's way more complicated than that.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's a good point, but there's also something to be said for the fact that many of that 1% &quot;believe&quot; in technology, and have been conditioned to expect rapid technological improvements as time goes on. That aside, my thing about the most faithful in religious communities operating as a good starting point is that they provide fields from which you might pluck your followers. The very devout are comfortable with activities that easily translate into a foci (prayer, mediation, active worship, reciting magic words, literal religious rituals)--if you can leverage some of the icons and symbols of their extant faith, you can slowly indoctrinate some of them. I'm wasn't trying to give the impression that I thought you can could just hijack Sunday mass and use them to summon Baphomet or something--only that, depending on what your magick looks like, there could be a religion with similar enough rituals and practices that you'd be well served in recruiting from those followers because the &quot;on ramp&quot; to our paradigm is probably less steep.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do not think that.<br /> <br /> I don't treat the rules as the reality of the game, I treat the rules as an approximation of the reality of the game. +5 isn't some new universal truth to the setting, it's just a different abstraction model of the setting. Whether a soft cap or a hard cap, the game is saying in the setting organizing too many people gets so hard the effort isn't worth it. It's just been phrased differently in moving from soft to hard.</div> </div> </div> </div> Respectfully, I read the rule as being as being a hard cap set by reality. For me the move from &quot;soft cap&quot; to &quot;hard cap&quot; is one of going from &quot;mechanically possible in setting, but reasonably unachievable by PCs&quot; to &quot;mechanically impossible&quot;. Particularly because of this bit at the end of the write-up for the Cult BG: &quot;It\u2019s possible to have a larger cult, of course \u2013 a popular evangelist might have hundreds of devotees. After a certain point, though, they can help you only so much. The five-dot rank in this Background offers the maximum benefit for a cult.&quot; This tells me the game acknowledges you could have a cult of hundreds, but nevertheless, you're not getting more out of them than the 5 dice. I certainly appreciate that you have another way of this reading this though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And it feels, to me, like you're letting that bug get to you more than it seems like you know you should.<br /> <br /> We get bugs up our asses, it happens. But sometimes the right move is for other people to say, &quot;dude, you've just got a bug up your ass about this, and it seems like just letting it go is the only option because once you do all this is going to fall away.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div> I mean, this whole thread is part of that. No one else has chimed in with any more satisfactory an explanation that I'd already considered, you've mentioned, or we've otherwise discussed. It is what it is, and that's all it is (I guess).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I've expressed that your explanations ring extremely hollow. It seriously feels like your attempts to explain why you're not persuaded are more justifications to not have to deal with them being persuasive positions. I'm not sure how you'd like me to go from there because all I can do is call that out if that's how I see it. I might have gotten exceedingly pissy about it last post, but the general point is one I still feel.</div> </div> </div> </div> I can only apologize for having frustrated you. From a nuts and bolts perspective, if you really believe in what you're saying to me, and that your position is imminently reasonable and obvious, you will naturally feel like I'm doing mental backflips to remain unpersuaded; but from my perspective, I've worked through the text and the possibilities (and arguments), and I'm unsatisfied. I agree that, realistically, it <i>must </i>be one of a small handful of things we've already discussed--and further, that there are facts at play here that I just don't know about that, but that if I <i>were </i>to know about, would make me feel/believe differently about this whole matter. Especially now having gone in circles so much, and you've effectively audited my thought process--but at least now, having gone through this exercise, I can just <i>deal </i>with not having an answer that's going to satisfy me. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was saying that this was always the intent of the rules, as born out by prior editions, even if not actualized by them by using a soft cap that could be subverted for anyone invested in using Cult to the maximum extent possible instead of a hard one.</div> </div> </div> </div> I agree with that. They've just now changed the rules in such a way so that, if you're going to run the run the game by the book, this just isn't in the toolbox of ST tricks to explain any given plot device or contrivance. Obviously there's always making something else up, but I really like having this as a go-to explanation; an internally consistent explanation for any given extraordinary or mind-blowing thing accomplished with True Magick, that was out of PC's reach in as satisfying and plausible a way as $1 billion being out of PC's reach (such that no one in any game I've ever played has even tried to make $1 billion, let alone felt slighted by hitting a wall in the attempt, because everyone properly appreciates how insanely difficult it would be, and unlikely to succeed).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there was a simple editing error were the Assistant writer used the older rules of adding successes and it didn't get changed to adding dice. That change alone makes it work with the rest of the book just fine.</div> </div> </div> </div> You're probably right. Having said that, I think it <i>does </i>demonstrate that the M20 conceived of Acolytes and Cultists as being two different sorts of people, with Acolytes being more competent and capable (dots on the page-wise), but with both categories seemingly have the same mechanical benefit for participating in rituals. By contrast, in Revised, I think the <i>Guide to the Traditions </i>write up on the Cult BG explicitly mentions Acolytes as being part of the Cult. This is the kind of rules change I tend to appreciate the most--where the setting gets built out; more nuance, more minutiae, more detail.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Confirmation bias towards your feelings of incongruity.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's probably true of all of this, down to the initial animus for making the thread in the first place--a desire to either have my 'take' confirmed, or to otherwise be shown incontrovertible evidence of my incorrectness.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382957#post1382957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Conceded, and hopefully improved on in your estimation.</div> </div> </div> </div> Thank you, and, yes, definitely.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-21T16:08:54-07:00","id":1383048,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1382988#post1382988\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I say, &quot;I don't know why they did X,&quot; and you offer as a possible explanation, &quot;to fix Problem Y,&quot; the degree to which it's either easy or hard for me to <i>buy </i>that explanation is going to be tied to the degree to which I saw or conceived of Problem Y as actually having been <i>a problem</i> in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div>The extent to which this has been a problem at my tables, and in general Mage discussions, is somewhat the opposite: most people seem to have no idea how potent this used to be. &quot;There's no way you can do that!&quot; &quot;Um, actually here, let me show you how anyone can do that...&quot; has happened more times than I can count. But that then runs into a design problem. If you want to make it much more likely people using the acting in concert rules (and acolytes/etc. via Cult means you automatically have style compatibility worked in that you don't with the other options), more people are going to realize how easy it actually is to cheese the system (or at least how ST dependent it is), creating a potential problem you might want to head off.<br /> <br /> This is what feels like the most likely scenario to me (esp. after the massive mechanical debacle of ritual magic in Mage the Awakening 1e when players figuring out how to abuse the shit of it that had to be addressed in that game's 2e). They wanted to make ritual casting a bigger deal (M20 has a much more extensive write-up for it), but also wanted to avoid the bonuses getting out of hand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because my issue is not, and has never been, that I thought this was realistically available to players and now it's not; it's a plot device mechanic--the place where I use the rule is to justify hand-wave-y things that far more powerful mages, or the Technocracy do. </div> </div> </div>I realized something that I think you might have overlooked.<br /> <br /> Previously you noted that M20 works like 2e: you get to pick one option. M20 shifted the un-enlightended helpers after the one option rule. That is, as written, you can pick between being helped by peers that get to roll, or mages that just a 1 success because they don't have enough magic to do more than that, and you can stack the dice bonus on top of that.<br /> <br /> So if pre-fall Porthos is getting a big ritual together, he has to pick between having the compatible members of his cabal and Chantry (not a bad choice, many of them are extremely high level mages in their own right and can easily toss in more than 1 success), or some number of younger Cabals working for him (which could be a large net of automatic bonus successes with less unpredictability to worry about). Then he gets +5 dice per roll on top of that from the servants of Doissetep he has access to. So he gets to roll a maximum 19 times, with 14 dice, with some rather large bucket of extra successes tacked on to the end of it. At difficulty 6 (a 5th rank effect, -3 max reduction) where he'd have a Sphere specialty that applies, he'd average 133 successes + the bonus successes from mages helping him + however much willpower he spent to extra successes (probably not many to aid in fending off ritual magic endurance).<br /> <br /> The only thing stopping him is the time/stamina rules, but even then it wouldn't be hard to to use this gathering to get everything blessed with buffed stamina and resistance to fatigue.<br /> <br /> If you're worried about the big hitters tossing out massive ritual successes... I hope this demonstrates that it's still eminently possible for them to do so with the M20 rules, and the hard cap there. On the &quot;rolling enough successes on the ritual rolls,&quot; side of things hitting 100 successes isn't even hard on average (the possibility of failing or botching individual rolls, and the stamina factor are the big deals here) for the huge powerhouses like Porthos.<br /> <br /> Given that math, &quot;How did they do XYX?&quot; &quot;Good stats.&quot; is still more than enough of an answer. The Technocracy can call on +20 successes worth of lower ranked amalgams and +5 dice worth of assistants, to help a high ranking member pull of a 30 success effect in 5-10 hours. The math works out fine, and the advantage there is the Technocratic machine letting them move specialists around to problem spots rapidly for this sort of thing where the Traditions would struggle to amass that sort of effort on short notice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Obviously if I can't work forward from the facts, as I understand them, to come up with a logical explanation for a rational person's actions, then I either have to conclude that (a) the person <i>isn't</i> rational (which would be an unwarranted and arrogant insult to Brucato and the other authors) <b>or </b>(b) that I simply have the facts wrong. </div> </div> </div>I think this is an unnecessarily restrictive approach. We're not talking about attempting to solve a crime mostly based on circumstantial evidence with no useful direct evidence. There isn't a single factual series of events to &quot;prove&quot; happened. Game design is an art, with decision making that is inherently not purely rational because it's a creative act. Trying to analyze an RPG on rational actors like this is inherently an issue when they're not written to be texts of pure rational discourse. Brucato didn't write anything for Mage in a purely rational way. That's not an insult, that's acknowledging that his work on Mage is a form of creative expression that means non-rational motivators factor into the whole process.<br /> <br /> Hell, humans aren't great rational actors in general. We're neurologically designed to rely on non-rational decision making. Being rational takes a lot of work, and the default scientific method relies on multiple people confirming the same things as a way to counteract that irrational decision making still leaks into such pursuits.<br /> <br /> The rules change could have been an aesthetic decision because Brucato likes it better this way (that's basically his defense of what I think is a shitty call to have Brawl, Martial Arts, and Do all in M20). That's not a purely rational decision (unarmed fighting having three Abilities, where hand-to-hand weapon fighting has one undercuts any argument that you need three t represent the variety of unarmed fighting), but it's not a &quot;wrong&quot; decision in the sense of there being wrong decisions from an aesthetic perspective (Brucato wants there to be a separation between street brawlers, students of dedication martial systems, and a magical super-fighting system). The facts aren't missing in this case, because he put the explanation in the BoS FAQ.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As an aside, I'd be interested to know what handful of things didn't get patched that you thought needed patching. For me, W20 is probably the best of the 20th anniversary games. I also happen to think the sun shines out of Bill Bridges' ass. </div> </div> </div>Oh, W20 is an overall net improvement for sure.<br /> <br /> That said?<br /> <br /> Do something to address the perception of the Red Talons as being unplayable in mixed groups (trying to be even-handed here, I think the Red Talons need to be completely rewritten but that's not an anniversary edition's job).<br /> The overly complicated soaking by form rules and the weird outcomes that occur (ex: Lupus can't spar in their breed form because they can't soak bite damage from other Garou while wolves, but the other Breeds can).<br /> The Purebreed and Rites Backgrounds. PB should have been split into multiple traits because it's doing two things at once, both poorly, and is exceedingly powerful for it. Rituals is a huge creation point sink for things that don't have costs to learn in play and there's a bunch of Rites that it's ridiculous Cliaths wouldn't be taught regardless of Tribe or Auspice.<br /> The roll for learning Rites forces any Rites heavy characters to start with Intelligence 4 or 5 or struggle constantly with their focus, which is already demanding on other Attributes.<br /> Gifts and Rites in general are largely bloated in number, and wildly inconsistent in utility/power. Do we need four different Rank 2 Gifts that give you armor dice? Why is Persuasion, one of the best social powers in the WoD, a Rank 1 Gift (in-splat for the most populous Breed, an Auspice, and two Tribes)? Is Combat Healing really powerful enough to be a level 3 Gift? etc.<br /> Fetish activation duration is still hit or miss on whether you get told or not.<br /> Totems desperately need create-your-own rules and the example Totems need to be written to follow them; esp. so it's easier to modify Totems with specific bans to something that's more relevant to the game your playing rather than forcing STs to create contreived situations so certain bans actually matter.<br /> Various confusing rules complications (ex: aborting actions in combat has two options and it's unclear how they're supposed to interact).<br /> <br /> Probably more but that's plenty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's a good point, but there's also something to be said for the fact that many of that 1% &quot;believe&quot; in technology, and have been conditioned to expect rapid technological improvements as time goes on. </div> </div> </div>My original comment on this tangent was that it would put the largest religions impact on Consensus as even with the Technocracy's push, not greater or incompatible with it (and that would be on top of the Order of Reason promoting religion for a long time before the secularization of the Union).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm wasn't trying to give the impression that I thought you can could just hijack Sunday mass and use them to summon Baphomet or something--only that, depending on what your magick looks like, there could be a religion with similar enough rituals and practices that you'd be well served in recruiting from those followers because the &quot;on ramp&quot; to our paradigm is probably less steep. </div> </div> </div>I think my issue was that's so... 101 I assumed you were going deeper than that. Mages of all stripes recruit from Sleeper cultures and subcultures that fit their styles already is a firm part of the game and always has been. My response was just that good recruiting grounds doesn't directly translate into lots of acolytes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Respectfully, I read the rule as being as being a hard cap set by reality. </div> </div> </div>Is this specific to this rule, or in general?<br /> <br /> Specific to this rule doesn't really feel like there's text in evidence to support that. The text says the cap is a matter of how much mechanical boost you can practically get, not a dictate from the universe.<br /> <br /> In general, I'm not sure how you maintain a coherent reading of the text. There are lots of caps in the rules that do no, at all, follow facts in our world, but are close enough for the purposes of the game. Treating them as dictates from the Mage reality that it works differently would just cause so many messes it seems entirely counterproductive to follow this approach. The top speed of a stock minivan is 120 mph? I have a speeding ticket that disagrees by 2 mph (funny story... the patrolman thought his radar gun was broken when he got that reading, but I owned up to being what my speedometer said too)... does that 2 mph really matter? Am I to believe that M20s setting a hard cap on how fast unmodified minivans can go set by reality itself despite knowing that cap isn't accurate? Or do I accept that isn't a functional way of reading the rules?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For me the move from &quot;soft cap&quot; to &quot;hard cap&quot; is one of going from &quot;mechanically possible in setting, but reasonably unachievable by PCs&quot; to &quot;mechanically impossible&quot;. </div> </div> </div>OK, but the move to dice means how this reflects on the reality of the game is different. Not being able to get more than +5 dice isn't the same as not being about to get more than +5 successes at the end of the ritual. If we're going to look at the reality in the game as reflected by the rules, you can't just focus on the hard cap in a vacuum.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Especially now having gone in circles so much, and you've effectively audited my thought process--but at least now, having gone through this exercise, I can just <i>deal </i>with not having an answer that's going to satisfy me. </div> </div> </div>I think that's a success at least, if minimal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're probably right. Having said that, I think it <i>does </i>demonstrate that the M20 conceived of Acolytes and Cultists as being two different sorts of people, with Acolytes being more competent and capable (dots on the page-wise), but with both categories seemingly have the same mechanical benefit for participating in rituals. By contrast, in Revised, I think the <i>Guide to the Traditions </i>write up on the Cult BG explicitly mentions Acolytes as being part of the Cult. This is the kind of rules change I tend to appreciate the most--where the setting gets built out; more nuance, more minutiae, more detail. </div> </div> </div>This was always a bit of a jumble. Ascensions Right Hand made it clear that mages have all sorts of non-mages working with them (something M20 G&amp;M has revisited now) but classifications of them throughout the editions was messy, esp. since they don't have inherent game distinctions.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-22T13:27:09-07:00","id":1383200,"title":"","content":"The balancing factor to using cult/unawakened acolytes from 2e onwards is that the rolls take a lot more time to do and that obviously you can't just...do that in the middle of a fight without some very creative sphere usage. And hey, if you've managed to get all those sleepers (hoping there's not a leaker/spy among them) and find a space to do an arduous long ritual for a buff, talisman or something of the sort...why shouldn't you, exactly?<br /> <br /> I think a lot of these discussions about how overpowered things are or can be in Mage are almost universally specific to white room situations. I'm not really convinced that Cult 'needed' a nerf, it comes across as sort of poorly thought out and a kneejerk balance meant to quell white room conversations on message boards than actual gameplay. You can't brush aside the narrative / logistical issues that you have to solve and optimise for as being irrelevant to the mechanical balance in a set of games where mechanics and narrative are encouraged to be interwoven.<br /> <br /> Also Cult as a Background didn't exist in 2e, you'd have to have used Guide to the Technocracy's allies above five (and be very specific) or used the Backup Background with 10 freebies to get 20 people and 4 automatic successes to the ritual.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26024-distant-choir","name":"Distant Choir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26024_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-22T14:02:17-07:00","id":1383211,"title":"","content":"In 2e (and Rev, and M20) you don't need the Backgrounds, the Backgrounds just make it easier. Having Cult 5 is a sign that your character has invested in the narrative and logistical issues already.<br /> <br /> A vampire doesn't need a Herd to get blood, Herd just makes it easier to feed by representing established methods of dealing with the issues of finding blood.<br /> <br /> In 2e, mages still had acolytes to help them out. The number of acolytes for Doissetep and Horizon previously cited are from 2e books after all. This thread had a discussion about how the white room theoretical +80 successes Porthos had access to by the 2e rules isn't really that in practice.<br /> <br /> Cult didn't need a nerf, because the issue wasn't Cult or Allies or whatever. You never and don't still need those to amass people for your ritual castings. It's how high that bonus gets at the 1 per 5 people rate.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-22T14:33:55-07:00","id":1383226,"title":"","content":"You said right out of the gate that it (cult) needed the nerf and that it was 'mind numbingly good' and I just set out to elucidate that the core concept did not need to be nerfed, there just needed to be guidance for ST's as how that pans out. <br /> <br /> Cult 5 is a sign that you've got people on hand to do it, but you still need to organise times (for them to come), space (for them to practice it) and duration (the time it takes the ritual to do) which are in it of itself balancing features that you don't actually get with the background and offer themselves up to a lot of complications once things get going.<br /> <br /> <br /> As for the acolyte discussion, I wasn't trying to set out that those things were impossible, just that system wise it's difficult to 'game' it without getting a narrative justification for it. The rules for players in that edition made it difficult because they hadn't really conceptualised a way to codify it like they did in Revised and then in M20.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26024-distant-choir","name":"Distant Choir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26024_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-22T15:04:54-07:00","id":1383230,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Distant Choir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383226#post1383226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You said right out of the gate that it (cult) needed the nerf and that it was 'mind numbingly good'....</div> </div> </div> </div>These are two distinct thoughts. Acting in concert with acolytes, which used to have no limit on how many successes it could generate (and no guidance on how many acolytes even exist), could generate game breaking numbers with no increase in risk.<br /> <br /> I called the Cult background mind numbingly good because it is. Even with a nerf to group rituals. It's cheap and has a huge bonus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ..and I just set out to elucidate that the core concept did not need to be nerfed, there just needed to be guidance for ST's as how that pans out. </div> </div> </div>The core concept not needing nerfing is a meaningless statement as the core concept isn't at issue. The issue is how far the older rules let you stretch the concept.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Cult 5 is a sign that you've got people on hand to do it, but you still need to organise times (for them to come), space (for them to practice it) and duration (the time it takes the ritual to do) which are in it of itself balancing features that you don't actually get with the background and offer themselves up to a lot of complications once things get going. </div> </div> </div>Again, none of this has anything to do with the changes M20 made. The move from a social roll to organize it all, to not having to roll out dealing with that and having a hard cap instead, is ultimate issue.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the acolyte discussion, I wasn't trying to set out that those things were impossible, just that system wise it's difficult to 'game' it without getting a narrative justification for it. The rules for players in that edition made it difficult because they hadn't really conceptualised a way to codify it like they did in Revised and then in M20. </div> </div> </div>The old rules were easier to game by virtue of not having anything but a narrative justification to make. If I can narratively justify 100 people, I get +20 successes. Boom. Done. That's all the gaming it takes. Rev. didn't change that by adding the Cult Background, it just codified an easy way to have a smaller number of people around instead of rounding up NPCs via whatever methods you came up with.<br /> <br /> You're making a claim that &quot;no rules makes it harder to do,&quot; which really needs more than an assertion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-23T06:14:45-07:00","id":1383352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383230#post1383230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> These are two distinct thoughts. Acting in concert with acolytes, which used to have no limit on how many successes it could generate (and no guidance on how many acolytes even exist), could generate game breaking numbers with no increase in risk.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is an assertion. That there was no system mechanism to enable the kind of scenario you're suggesting (in the case of 2e, reread my posts pertaining to this) means that you must rely on the narrative, which in any case leads to the risks I've mentioned previously. Now, let's say we sweep aside the problems of 1) getting say a 100 people 2) getting them to a location like a Sanctum or other place which will ensure reductions in difficulty without the need to burn a lot of Quintessence or to make numerous ability enhancing magick roles 3) making sure none of them are moles or have been compromised (whether they're spies or if they're not actually stringent believers) 4) passing any necessary rolls outside of the magic casting itself 5) ensuring that for the duration of the long ritual that it won't be interrupted 6) setting out a practical goal within the purview of your vastly gained resources. It's sort of misrepresentation of how things will actually play out (since you need to take them into account), but as I say, there's a point here.<br /> <br /> Alright, all of that is swept aside and we're left with the outcome where Porthos or another politically and metaphysically powerful Mage can start tossing out huge effects with lots of successes...and drawing the attention of their enemies who operate under the same rules as this hypothetical charismatic Mage. I think that's the world narrative working as intended. Even from the Book of Mirrors there's literally a cold war between all the powerful archmages of the main two factions. There are risks to using these huge resources, they're balanced within the narrative of the world as well as the system we have at hand. <br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383230#post1383230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I called the Cult background mind numbingly good because it is. Even with a nerf to group rituals. It's cheap and has a huge bonus.</div> </div> </div> </div>It has a big resource cost (aside from freebies). Great Works tend to take a long time between rolls to do so you\u2019re otherwise having to dedicate yourself toward it or taking steps to mitigate it. Limited application because of the amount of time / logistics it takes (so no fireballs in a quick pinch unless you took higher spheres levels to ensure it). I think it\u2019s a good background, but you said it had to nerfed because it was mind numbingly good. I am holding you to that statement because I think it\u2019s ill informed.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383230#post1383230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The core concept not needing nerfing is a meaningless statement as the core concept isn't at issue. The issue is how far the older rules let you stretch the concept.</div> </div> </div> </div>You\u2019ve been arguing that the core concept -did- need a nerf. The core concept is \u2018more people = more magic effects you can pull\u2019 in the most basic, fundamental level. And now there\u2019s a cap. That\u2019s a nerf to the core concept whether or not you agree that it was necessary. Please, reread my post.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383230#post1383230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, none of this has anything to do with the changes M20 made. The move from a social roll to organize it all, to not having to roll out dealing with that and having a hard cap instead, is ultimate issue.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is kind of misleading. Revised\u2019s Cult Background, as the OP so dutifully pointed out, is exactly the same as M20\u2019s Cult except the background\u2019s system itself is a hard cap. Revised\u2019s core book did not indicate a hard cap to unawakened assistants acting in concert with the Cult Background, it deferred completely to the core book where that cap didn\u2019t exist. The ultimate issue here is that there\u2019s a cap to your unawakened assistants in general, one that happens to coincide with Cult as a guideline.<br /> <br /> An issue, might I add, that hasn\u2019t really been satisfied by your arguments.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383230#post1383230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The old rules were easier to game by virtue of not having anything but a narrative justification to make. If I can narratively justify 100 people, I get +20 successes. Boom. Done. That's all the gaming it takes. Rev. didn't change that by adding the Cult Background, it just codified an easy way to have a smaller number of people around instead of rounding up NPCs via whatever methods you came up with.</div> </div> </div> </div>I recognise that you\u2019ve been in the tabletop community for a long while, especially involved in discussions for World Of Darkness. It\u2019s this reason that makes me really question your rather blaise and I\u2019d even say disingenuous assertion that narrative justification is something of a non-issue. See above for the issues you\u2019re going to run into if it\u2019s a purely narrative affair. In my eyes and in my experience of playing tabletop, it\u2019s a lot easier to game systems than it is to game narratives. I have to imagine that for someone as obviously experienced in these things that you too must recognise that.<br /> <br /> <br /> Apologies if the formatting is off, this website really doesn't like you using its interface for more than a few minutes, lol.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26024-distant-choir","name":"Distant Choir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26024_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-23T19:21:06-07:00","id":1383498,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Distant Choir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383352#post1383352\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That there was no system mechanism to enable the kind of scenario you're suggesting (in the case of 2e, reread my posts pertaining to this) means that you must rely on the narrative, which in any case leads to the risks I've mentioned previously.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, but this whole argument is one giant Rule Zero fallacy that tries to hide behind &quot;the narrative,&quot; as if that's not just GM fiat.<br /> <br /> You're saying things like &quot;you'd have to deal with X.&quot; Is there a rule about that in the game? No. That means you're arguing that you would make players deal with X despite X not actually being required by the game. That's not a defense of the rules-as-written, but saying the RAW don't matter because the GM will fix it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think it\u2019s a good background, but you said it had to nerfed because it was mind numbingly good. I am holding you to that statement because I think it\u2019s ill informed. </div> </div> </div>I said the acting in concert rules needed to be nerfed. Cult only needed to be changed to make it fit with the appropriate nerfing acting in concert.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u200bYou\u2019ve been arguing that the core concept -did- need a nerf. The core concept is \u2018more people = more magic effects you can pull\u2019 in the most basic, fundamental level. </div> </div> </div>Are you dense or just trolling?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It\u2019s this reason that makes me really question your rather blaise and I\u2019d even say disingenuous assertion that narrative justification is something of a non-issue. </div> </div> </div>Oh we're doing this sort of shit.<br /> <br /> Tell me, is your overtly spurious attempts at a debate just a low post count user trying to take down a long standing user for some imagined sense of gaining &quot;points&quot; or cred?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have to imagine that for someone as obviously experienced in these things that you too must recognise that. </div> </div> </div>Ooooo, I love being told that I must recognize that you're clearly perfect and awesome and correct in all things because my experience with the game is just a rhetorical football for you to kick around.<br /> <br /> And no. I don't recognize this as true at all.<br /> <br /> You can't game &quot;the maximum bonus is +5.&quot; That's the maximum bonus. Period. There's no way to manipulate it to really get +6 or higher.<br /> <br /> You can game the narrative as easily as you can game your GM. I've seen people gain six Background dots in a single session because (most) Backgrounds raise purely on narrative events, and they out thought the GM into having all those dots handed to them because the GM couldn't argue that the narrative didn't result in them.<br /> <br /> You can't do that with things that cost XP, because no matter how well you work your character into a position where gaining a bunch of different things make sense by the narrative, you can't buy more than your XP allows and you just pick which things increased and accept that you didn't improve in everything that might improve after an appropriate narrative situation.<br /> <br /> The whole point of rules in RPGs is to resolve the narrative in a way that's not just down to one person's fiat/whim.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T02:46:12-07:00","id":1383544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383498#post1383498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sorry, but this whole argument is one giant Rule Zero fallacy that tries to hide behind &quot;the narrative,&quot; as if that's not just GM fiat.<br /> <br /> You're saying things like &quot;you'd have to deal with X.&quot; Is there a rule about that in the game? No. That means you're arguing that you would make players deal with X despite X not actually being required by the game. That's not a defense of the rules-as-written, but saying the RAW don't matter because the GM will fix it.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Is there a rule that says when you start fighting in a busy street that the cops show up? Is there a set of mechanical rules for a situation when factions or organisations don\u2019t like you? This isn\u2019t a wargame, the game is designed to allow narrative to alter how some mechanics play out. And by the way, 2e/Revised/m20 do bring up these issues in the text.<br /> <br /> You\u2019re conflating with adding narrative to a narrative game with making up rules. It\u2019s dishonest. It\u2019s being framed as if there\u2019s no story or setting accompanying the system, when that\u2019s not how the community as a whole plays the game. If you think it needed a nerf because of white room discussions on Spacebattles or something\u2026I have no idea what to tell you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383498#post1383498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I said the acting in concert rules needed to be nerfed. Cult only needed to be changed to make it fit with the appropriate nerfing acting in concert.</div> </div> </div> </div>You said Cult needed a nerf. If you\u2019re willing to retract that statement, I\u2019m fine with that. As for whether acting in concert needed a nerf\u2026well, you haven\u2019t really responded to my counter arguments in that without obfuscating the discussion between what is \u2018RAW\u2019 and what isn\u2019t. Are the narrative considerations that the books literally tell you as a player/ST not RAW? Come on.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383498#post1383498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are you dense or just trolling?</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering that thus far you haven\u2019t really mustered up a good counter-argument, I have to say that I\u2019m not the one trolling here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383498#post1383498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh we're doing this sort of shit.<br /> <br /> Tell me, is your overtly spurious attempts at a debate just a low post count user trying to take down a long standing user for some imagined sense of gaining &quot;points&quot; or cred?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>You\u2019re clearly experienced with tabletops and especially this game, so this kind of odd white room inclination that is more present to people newer to the system is really making me scratch my head. Why the need to mythologise it in this way?<br /> <br /> And honestly, I don\u2019t think I\u2019d get much street cred out of \u2018taking you down\u2019 whatever that means. I\u2019m not sure you\u2019re as famous as you think.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383498#post1383498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ooooo, I love being told that I must recognize that you're clearly perfect and awesome and correct in all things because my experience with the game is just a rhetorical football for you to kick around.<br /> <br /> And no. I don't recognize this as true at all.<br /> <br /> You can't game &quot;the maximum bonus is +5.&quot; That's the maximum bonus. Period. There's no way to manipulate it to really get +6 or higher.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> You can game the narrative as easily as you can game your GM. I've seen people gain six Background dots in a single session because (most) Backgrounds raise purely on narrative events, and they out thought the GM into having all those dots handed to them because the GM couldn't argue that the narrative didn't result in them.<br /> <br /> You can't do that with things that cost XP, because no matter how well you work your character into a position where gaining a bunch of different things make sense by the narrative, you can't buy more than your XP allows and you just pick which things increased and accept that you didn't improve in everything that might improve after an appropriate narrative situation.<br /> <br /> The whole point of rules in RPGs is to resolve the narrative in a way that's not just down to one person's fiat/whim.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not going to dissect this too deeply since I think there\u2019s a little bit of a contradiction in your expectations.<br /> If a GM is so totally compromised that he\u2019s willing to give 6 background dots in a session (this is assuming the situation didn\u2019t make sense, if it did\u2026what\u2019s the problem?) and bend the rules in fantastical ways, what exactly was stopping him from tweaking the cap to begin with?<br /> <br /> In a roleplaying game like this, the experience is negotiated. There are rules, you\u2019re right. Rules in many ways are good. They make the experience more interesting and are vehicles for the narrative in this context. But this rule is not good. It assumes a rather fantastical white room and overestimates its viability. Even within the context of m20 where the acting in concert rule is changed, they tell you outright of the narrative implications that complicate the story (a fact you have ignored)<br /> <br /> Nearly every core book has in the back advice that tells STs either to change the rules if they don\u2019t make sense (Not applicable here) or to elucidate narrative complications from certain rules being used (Applicable).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26024-distant-choir","name":"Distant Choir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26024_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T04:43:14-07:00","id":1383561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Distant Choir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383544#post1383544\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there a rule that says when you start fighting in a busy street that the cops show up?</div> </div> </div> </div>No. Which is why &quot;the cops might show up!&quot; doesn't matter to discussion about if Dexterity is too powerful in the combat rules.<br /> <br /> Do you know what the Rule Zero fallacy is?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is there a set of mechanical rules for a situation when factions or organisations don\u2019t like you? </div> </div> </div>Some yes actually. Backgrounds, Merits, and Flaws all have rules for being liked or disliked by factions and organizations.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This isn\u2019t a wargame, the game is designed to allow narrative to alter how some mechanics play out. And by the way, 2e/Revised/m20 do bring up these issues in the text. </div> </div> </div>If you think wargames never have these sorts of things... you might want to play some instead of assuming things about them. Wargamers might love to pit their best armies against each other to refine their tactics, but wargaming as a hobby is full of narrative scenarios that can alter all sorts of things (ex: there was a Mechwarrior Dark Ages tournament scenario that involved two teams air dropping at the same time, that had variant combat rules for mechs fighting during freefall, custom unit dispersal rules on landing, and a variant capture-the-flag victory condition to make up for all the odd situation of the opening narrative).<br /> <br /> But, again, it doesn't matter if the narrative can alter how some mechanics play out. The ability for the GM to alter the situation so a bad rule doesn't ruin the fun of the game doesn't make the rule good. The GM's tool being the narrative doesn't change this.<br /> <br /> If the only defense of a rule is, &quot;but the narrative will keep it in check,&quot; than there's no defense of the rule, because the narrative is ephemeral in this context.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You\u2019re conflating with adding narrative to a narrative game with making up rules. </div> </div> </div>I'm saying that using GM fiat to add narrative obstacles that have to be overcome to make it look like a rule is less powerful than it is, is making up rules. Because it is making up rules.<br /> <br /> Though it's rich that someone that's having a lot of trouble with keeping facts straight is trying to call me the dishonest one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It\u2019s being framed as if there\u2019s no story or setting accompanying the system, when that\u2019s not how the community as a whole plays the game. </div> </div> </div>First off, it's not being framed that way at all, and claiming it has been is a complete and utter lie. There was a whole discussion centered around the context of the rules in the setting. Specific setting locations and the characters associated with them were analyzed. Multiple posts contextualize the rules in the setting. So, you know, don't call me dishonest when you're objectively lying about the thread.<br /> <br /> Second, the community doesn't play the game in one fashion, not right now, not over approaching 30 years of the game existing. There are people that play to craft a narrative, and there are people that play for immersive sandbox styles where narrative is only an emergent by-product, and lots of people in between somewhere. There are people that play fast and loose with the rules, and people that are sticklers for every rule in the book. Etc.<br /> <br /> Of course, if you really cared about a serious discussion of this? The White Room is not the enemy. It's a starting point. Because everyone's narrative (and approach to how narrative happens) is different, the White Room's place is to set the base-line. What do the rules do without everyone's biases seeping in? It's useful to know, because it gives an objective point for people to refer back to. If people can't even agree on what happens in the White Room, there's a bigger problem to handle before dealing with everyone's setting preferences.<br /> <br /> For example, you keep bring up some sort of spies as an important factor in this. They're not to me, because they're going to stand out like an out of tune instrument in a band. Group casting has to harmonize around magical principles. A Technocratic agent that can assist in Enlightened procedures might be able to infiltrate a Tradition Chantry, but their lack of belief in how the magic works for a given Tradition mage is going to single them out fast if they set themselves up to be part of a ritual casting session. If they're trained in how not to get caught, they're going to avoid entering into this equation, because it's going to blow their cover (because they're going to get caught reaaaaaly fast if suspected, mages are really good at telling if your lying, or if you have magic shielding your thoughts).<br /> <br /> But you seem to feel they really need to matter to this. I doubt we're going to come to some agreement on it, so either we can (a) not discuss the actual mechanic at all or (b) ignore that difference in how we build up the setting when we're playing, leading the discussion closer to the White Room. Hence the whole, &quot;White Room bad, narrative, narrative, setting, narrative&quot; routine is actually unhelpful at best. It turns into debates on what the narrative should be, rather than if the rule is actually good at what it's meant to do. We need to resort to the White Room if we're going to avoid that, because we don't have the time to explore the infinite variations of narrative possible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you think it needed a nerf because of white room discussions on Spacebattles or something\u2026I have no idea what to tell you. </div> </div> </div>If you think ad hominem barbs and strawmen are doing anything but making you look even more dishonest... I have no idea what to tell you that shouldn't already be clear.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You said Cult needed a nerf. If you\u2019re willing to retract that statement, I\u2019m fine with that. </div> </div> </div>I don't need to retract something you're making up for whatever reason you seem to think is necessary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for whether acting in concert needed a nerf\u2026well, you haven\u2019t really responded to my counter arguments in that without obfuscating the discussion between what is \u2018RAW\u2019 and what isn\u2019t. </div> </div> </div>Your <b>only and single</b> counterargument is that it didn't need the nerf because narrative will constrain it; which I have responded to in depth. You're the one obfuscating the discussion with strawmen and lies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Considering that thus far you haven\u2019t really mustered up a good counter-argument, I have to say that I\u2019m not the one trolling here. </div> </div> </div>You haven't even mustered up a good argument. Countering &quot;but narrative!&quot; was easily done.<br /> <br /> And you appear like a troll, because you seem more interested in bashing the White Room (which wasn't even a significant issue in the discussion until you came in and made it one), than having an good faith chat about things.<br /> <br /> But sure, project more.<br /> <br /> Also remember that you're the one that decided to make this about each other, and not just the game, as well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You\u2019re clearly experienced with tabletops and especially this game, so this kind of odd white room inclination that is more present to people newer to the system is really making me scratch my head. </div> </div> </div>Well, besides all my experience with the game... my experience with online discussions is that White Room talk is by far not some newbie slanted thing. I have no idea where you'd get that impression, because over lots of forums, chatrooms, and so on, it's usually the old hands that jump into the White Room at the drop of a hat, not the new folks. Maybe the issue, giving the benefit of the doubt that you're actually scratching your head on this, is you need to read more online RPG talk?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why the need to mythologise it in this way? </div> </div> </div>You're. Making. This. Up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And honestly, I don\u2019t think I\u2019d get much street cred out of \u2018taking you down\u2019 whatever that means. </div> </div> </div>I've seen lots of people treat forums like the cliche of being new to a prison and beating up someone to prove you're tough. I've been told directly by a couple people that were trying to win a debate with me specifically to show they should be shown respect even though nobody was actually disrespecting them until their bad faith was obvious. You're giving me those vibes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I\u2019m not sure you\u2019re as famous as you think. </div> </div> </div>Oh, it's not at all about fame, it's about the number under the name that seems to set people off. I'm just the big number that happened to be here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If a GM is so totally compromised that he\u2019s willing to give 6 background dots in a session (this is assuming the situation didn\u2019t make sense, if it did\u2026what\u2019s the problem?) and bend the rules in fantastical ways, what exactly was stopping him from tweaking the cap to begin with? </div> </div> </div>First, never said there was a problem, just that narrative constraints are easier to game than mechanical restraints. Though if you'd like me to elaborate on the issues of why narrative granted traits bought with the same pool of points as traits that require limited mechanical resources to acquire I can do so easily. Or you could go to the VtM forum as ask &quot;Why was Generation being a Background a bad thing?&quot; if you want a lot of people to give the same answer.<br /> <br /> Second, no rules were bent or broken.<br /> <br /> Third, what cap would she be tweaking? There's no cap on how many dots in Backgrounds you can get during a session. There's only a cap on how many you can get in a single Background at a time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In a roleplaying game like this, the experience is negotiated. </div> </div> </div>If you mean XP, not it isn't negotiated.<br /> <br /> If you mean some vague concept of the experience of playing the game... it probably should be but it isn't nearly as much as you seem to think.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But this rule is not good. </div> </div> </div>Just saying it doesn't make it true. You have yet to give a single reason why nerfing acting in concert is a bad rule. I've already established that the one thing it supposedly cut out of the narrative tool kit (big guns bring big effects) is actually still firmly in place. You've offered no countered to my points about how moving it to a dice bonus instead of a success bonus actually helps highlight the narrative around it. You're just bitching about the White Room and tossing out half-baked assertions that never make a point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (a fact you have ignored) </div> </div> </div>No, just something I didn't feel like repeating because you want to cherry pick from things I said on the first page of the thread.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nearly every core book has in the back advice that tells STs either to change the rules if they don\u2019t make sense (Not applicable here) or to elucidate narrative complications from certain rules being used (Applicable). </div> </div> </div>So, right, Rule Zero fallacy again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 04-24-2020, 07:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T09:14:50-07:00","id":1383611,"title":"","content":"I think we've covered the original question well. The original question was, 'why was the acolyte contribution to rituals nerfed?' While we don't have a definitive answer from the designers of Mage about the changes to the use of acolytes for magic and any changes to the Cult background, having helped on the new edition of HackMaster and serving on the HackMaster War College for the AD&amp;D 2e licensed version, I have often had to deal with the issue of the Rules As Written and what the intent was.<br /> <br /> Specifically, when a rule or statement in the game material didn't make sense to someone and they wanted to have an errata correct it, we had to recommend to the game's creators one of three options:<br /> 1. The person wanting the correction didn't understand the rule or the intent. The recommendation was the rule should stand as is.<br /> 2. The writing as it stands is unclear and needs a correction because it impacts play. We asked for and suggested a correction.<br /> 3. There's a typographical error here or the rule is more clearly explained later with slightly different terminology. In this case, we recommended that the errata reflect the clearer interpretation.<br /> <br /> After this experience for a game that generated a 20-page errata sheet for official play, we got Rule 1 in the current edition of HackMaster.<br /> <br /> 'In HackMaster, any rule ambiguity related to character creation and PC powers is construed against the player character. If you, as a player, find yourself arguing that a rule is<br /> ambiguous, your GM must simply weigh both side's benefits to your player character and choose the most logical choice in his opinion. If one choice seems too heavily in favor of your PC and not directly stated in the rules, he has no choice but to rule against your character' HackMaster Player's Handbook, page 9. The point of that rule, since there were serious rules lawyering problems in the prior edition of HackMaster and the nature of organized play made any rules problem a play problem, is that the designers' work is presumed to be right and that to go beyond the rules doesn't fit the game. It's a restrictive interpretation--the legalistic formulation when you read it is fits the fact one of the main writers is a lawyer in real life.<br /> <br /> While you can play a game any way you and your friends wish outside of an organized play environment, the game designer wrote the rules to create a specific game framework. The game designer set the narrative framework with their rules and fiction. Their rules and fiction work within that framework and making changes can alter the play experience substantially.<br /> <br /> As such, while I do house rule a few things in Mage, I am fully aware that starting a character with 3 Arete instead of 1 (also capped Arete at 3 until improved in play) and giving out a few more freebie points isn't going to be as big of a change as changing the basic rules for magic by changing how group casting works. When we don't like rules, we should carefully consider how changing them changes the narrative.<br /> <br /> That's my 2 cents. . . <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T12:35:00-07:00","id":1383654,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383611#post1383611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">having helped on the new edition of HackMaster and serving on the HackMaster War College for the AD&amp;D 2e licensed version, I have often had to deal with the issue of the Rules As Written and what the intent was.<br /> <br /> Specifically, when a rule or statement in the game material didn't make sense to someone and they wanted to have an errata correct it, we had to recommend to the game's creators one of three options:<br /> 1. The person wanting the correction didn't understand the rule or the intent. The recommendation was the rule should stand as is.<br /> 2. The writing as it stands is unclear and needs a correction because it impacts play. We asked for and suggested a correction.<br /> 3. There's a typographical error here or the rule is more clearly explained later with slightly different terminology. In this case, we recommended that the errata reflect the clearer interpretation.</div> </div> </div> </div> I think the situation we have with the change to the Acolyte rule is distinguishable from the three scenarios above, because it does not appear to have been born of any ambiguity or scrivener's error in the rules. The possibilities here either: first, you can say the designers of the old rule had somehow either not considered the practical implications of what they were writing (i.e., that if you get 100 Acolytes together, as written you can get 20 automatic successes), which is something I have a hard time believing in anything but an academic vacuum; <i>or </i>second, they understood the implications of what they had written, but <u>initially</u> assumed that STs would naturally curb the ability to get a lot of Acolytes via what Heavy Arms and I dubbed 'soft' factors (like the social difficulty in charming and coordinating that number of participants), but only later (while writing M20) determined that this was not strongly-worded enough, or still left too much room for abuse, and put on the hard cap. Notably, Heavy Arms later wrote on to explain that the new system also has a great potential for &quot;breaking the game&quot; if you consider that you can now potentially amass 20 additional successes from just 25 Acolytes by dint of the fact that they add 5 dice to every roll you make in the ritual, rather than just tacking on a static number of successes at the end based on the exchange rate.<br /> <br /> I digress, there's one very easy way to determine that the rule wasn't actually ambiguous: if you had only the 2nd Ed. and Revised Core books, you really wouldn't be able to come up with an interpretation of those rules that there's a cap on the contributions that can be made--much less a cap of 5. Certainly <i>now</i> that they've changed the rule, we can infer that there was something unsatisfactory about it to the designers (because otherwise they wouldn't have changed it), and we can try to explain the change by assuming they originally made a mistake, or meant to do something else. However, the problem with the rule never announced itself, which is to say that it's not like there was anything on the face of the rule that would have confused anyone about how many successes they would have added to their ritual if they had gotten 30, 50, or 65 Acolytes together for a ritual.We wouldn't even have known there was anything wrong, or even competing theories as to the intent of the rule, if they hadn't first changed the rule for M20--which was what prompted my question and this thread--that I wasn't aware of anyone ever having complained about the rule being abused, or of any meta-discussion about differences in opinion on how the rule should operate, or of competing interpretations. The second part of our discussion above was because I had worked forward from the rule-as-written (as well as some assorted other things in the setting) and inferred that getting a shit load of Acolytes together to do ridiculous stuff was a next-level flex used by plot device NPC's to do things like create giant realms, or whatever other outlandish thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383611#post1383611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In HackMaster, any rule ambiguity related to character creation and PC powers is construed against the player character. If you, as a player, find yourself arguing that a rule is ambiguous, your GM must simply weigh both side's benefits to your player character and choose the most logical choice in his opinion. If one choice seems too heavily in favor of your PC and not directly stated in the rules, he has no choice but to rule against your character' HackMaster Player's Handbook, page 9. The point of that rule, since there were serious rules lawyering problems in the prior edition of HackMaster and the nature of organized play made any rules problem a play problem, is that the designers' work is presumed to be right and that to go beyond the rules doesn't fit the game. It's a restrictive interpretation--the legalistic formulation when you read it is fits the fact one of the main writers is a lawyer in real life.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm also an attorney--not surprised at all that that rule was written by a lawyer. The common law rule with regard to ambiguities in contracts is that when a provision is subject to two or more reasonable interpretations, the provision will be construed strictly against the drafter. The idea is that the drafter is in a superior position to choose wording by dint of being the party who initially formulated whatever language. Ambiguous provisions of insurance policies (which are contracts after all) are also construed strictly in favor of coverage--the insurance carrier being in a superior position to the insured, inasmuch as they drafted the policy, deliberated upon the language, and ostensibly knew what they were doing when they put pen to paper. The legal rule is obviously concerned with traditional notions of fairness: if you get the draft the language, it's only fair that you should be bound by it, otherwise you could continually change the terms on the other party by saying any given benefit for themselves they find is the result of a misinterpretation, because your intentions were actually otherwise. In other words, it's interesting (and a little funny) that the people who wrote this rule did so knowing it to be <i>fundamentally </i>unfair--and actually kinda futile...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383611#post1383611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While you can play a game any way you and your friends wish outside of an organized play environment, the game designer wrote the rules to create a specific game framework. The game designer set the narrative framework with their rules and fiction. Their rules and fiction work within that framework and making changes can alter the play experience substantially.</div> </div> </div> </div>Granted, but it's still just &quot;The Golden Rule&quot; with more steps. No matter how much procedure you hang around it, the rule still requires that someone exercise a Judge's fiat to determine first, whether a given rule is or isn't ambiguous (ambiguity being defined as being subject to two or more reasonable, conflicting interpretations); and then to choose which is <i>most logical</i>. The issue here is that whether an interpretation is &quot;reasonable&quot; (and therefore triggers the ambiguity rule) is not <i>technically </i>a matter that is objectively determinable. What is reasonable and logical is ultimately an 'eye of the beholder' problem. So even if during organized play, the GM wants to fall back on this neat little procedural trick, the issue that arises is that the player may say &quot;well, this isn't ambiguous, because this rule we're talking about is only subject to <i>one </i>reasonable interpretation.&quot; At that point, the GM can <i>only</i> say &quot;that's not for you to determine&quot; and exercise their plenary authority at the table to do whatever it is they're going to do. Same for the later part of the analysis re- &quot;logical&quot;; a player could say &quot;well <i>that's</i> not the most logical choice at all,&quot; to which the GM would say, &quot;in my opinion, it is--moving on...&quot;<br /> <br /> My point is that you can dress it up however you want--the solution to incessant rules lawyering that slows down play is <i>always</i> going to be to exercise the golden rule, and (or) simply not play with that person at all. Because you're <i>not </i>going to find some bit of wording that eliminates completely the need for the GM to say (and remind everyone) that they have the discretion to do literally whatever they want to in the game, and that theirs is the only discretion that matters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T13:12:00-07:00","id":1383664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383611#post1383611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When we don't like rules, we should carefully consider how changing them changes the narrative.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ultimately though, this doesn't really change the narrative in a significant way; esp. for the default play character levels the game establishes. It changes a some of the odds of some of the potential outcomes of the narrative.<br /> <br /> The narrative of amassing un-enlightended helpers for your magic is still in place. The narrative of there being a complications for it is still in place. The narrative that it can go wrong horribly wrong from all those people trying to act as one is still in place. The narrative that you can get massive effects beyond the resolution of the standard rules is still in place.<br /> <br /> I would argue (and did and all) that the narrative is actually stronger because making the help dice instead of bonus successes highlights the risks involved in a more concrete way for player experience than jumping through the hoops of arranging a bunch of NPCs properly. Putting the primary risk into the the uncertainty of the ritual roll itself means that when your ritual explodes in your face, it might suck, but it was the risk you took for trying such a big ritual. Having your ritual either fail or under perform because you didn't roll well enough on your High Ritual check and your social rolls to lead your assistants combined (or worse things like a GM saying, &quot;oh you didn't say you were doing X, so Y goes wrong&quot;) can make players feel disconnected from the risk and feel like they failed because of GM antagonism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T14:42:14-07:00","id":1383672,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383664#post1383664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would argue (and did and all) that the narrative is actually stronger because making the help dice instead of bonus successes highlights the risks involved in a more concrete way for player experience than jumping through the hoops of arranging a bunch of NPCs properly. Putting the primary risk into the the uncertainty of the ritual roll itself means that when your ritual explodes in your face, it might suck, but it was the risk you took for trying such a big ritual. Having your ritual either fail or under perform because you didn't roll well enough on your High Ritual check and your social rolls to lead your assistants combined (or worse things like a GM saying, &quot;oh you didn't say you were doing X, so Y goes wrong&quot;) can make players feel disconnected from the risk and feel like they failed because of GM antagonism.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm still wrapping my head around the fact that you would, using M20, get an additional 5 dice per roll if using the maximum Cult BG during rituals. Assuming that you have a base Arete of 3, and you also bought some high Sanctum, there is some pretty wild stuff you could pull off while greatly mitigating the statistical likelihood of a botch--and it sort of democratizes the potentially OP application of the background. It shouldn't be a gimme that you can just maintain a Rank 5 Cult, but it should definitely be doable. Based on my experiments using rolz.com, it really seems like you could probably get 20 successes out of your Cult 5 -- which would previously have required the vaunted 100 Cultists, in like 4-5 rolls (still made difficult by the fact that represents between 20 and 25 total hours of ritual), but definitely not out of the question; particularly if you're taking breaks.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T15:07:19-07:00","id":1383677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No. Which is why &quot;the cops might show up!&quot; doesn't matter to discussion about if Dexterity is too powerful in the combat rules.<br /> <br /> Do you know what the Rule Zero fallacy is?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The fallacy fallacy. The books tell you these narrative considerations in explicit reference to Cult and working in concert.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some yes actually. Backgrounds, Merits, and Flaws all have rules for being liked or disliked by factions and organizations.</div> </div> </div> </div>Many, if not the majority of those considerations are very specific to a particular faction or individual. Do you think if those merits didn\u2019t exist that it couldn\u2019t happen? Hint: \u2018rules zero fallacy\u2019 is once again not applicable.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you think wargames never have these sorts of things... you might want to play some instead of assuming things about them. Wargamers might love to pit their best armies against each other to refine their tactics, but wargaming as a hobby is full of narrative scenarios that can alter all sorts of things (ex: there was a Mechwarrior Dark Ages tournament scenario that involved two teams air dropping at the same time, that had variant combat rules for mechs fighting during freefall, custom unit dispersal rules on landing, and a variant capture-the-flag victory condition to make up for all the odd situation of the opening narrative).<br /> <br /> But, again, it doesn't matter if the narrative can alter how some mechanics play out. The ability for the GM to alter the situation so a bad rule doesn't ruin the fun of the game doesn't make the rule good. The GM's tool being the narrative doesn't change this.<br /> <br /> If the only defense of a rule is, &quot;but the narrative will keep it in check,&quot; than there's no defense of the rule, because the narrative is ephemeral in this context.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>You\u2019re sort of proving my point for me, since those things you mentioned have specific rules applied to them because of the nature of what a Wargame is compared to a ttrpg like World of Darkness. World of Darkness does not provide specific minutiae for every little detail because the game outright tells you at multiple points that it comes down to your tables need and what you think would work for the scenario.<br /> <br /> Not to say that what I\u2019ve been arguing thus far is so out there in terms of rules as to be otherwise unapplicable.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm saying that using GM fiat to add narrative obstacles that have to be overcome to make it look like a rule is less powerful than it is, is making up rules. Because it is making up rules.<br /> <br /> Though it's rich that someone that's having a lot of trouble with keeping facts straight is trying to call me the dishonest one.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, the cops showing up if you run outside and shoot people in a campaign in a crowded city is not a rule. It\u2019s dishonest that you are simultaneously claiming there is no consideration in game mechanics to these issues when the books <u>flat out tell you otherwise</u>. My facts have been straight thus far, but I\u2019m seriously questioning as to whether this is wilful ignorance or just a miscommunication; can you actually respond to this point without resorting to your poorly applied \u2018Rule Zero Fallacy\u2019<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First off, it's not being framed that way at all, and claiming it has been is a complete and utter lie. There was a whole discussion centered around the context of the rules in the setting. Specific setting locations and the characters associated with them were analyzed. Multiple posts contextualize the rules in the setting. So, you know, don't call me dishonest when you're objectively lying about the thread.<br /> <br /> Second, the community doesn't play the game in one fashion, not right now, not over approaching 30 years of the game existing. There are people that play to craft a narrative, and there are people that play for immersive sandbox styles where narrative is only an emergent by-product, and lots of people in between somewhere. There are people that play fast and loose with the rules, and people that are sticklers for every rule in the book. Etc.<br /> <br /> Of course, if you really cared about a serious discussion of this? The White Room is not the enemy. It's a starting point. Because everyone's narrative (and approach to how narrative happens) is different, the White Room's place is to set the base-line. What do the rules do without everyone's biases seeping in? It's useful to know, because it gives an objective point for people to refer back to. If people can't even agree on what happens in the White Room, there's a bigger problem to handle before dealing with everyone's setting preferences.<br /> <br /> For example, you keep bring up some sort of spies as an important factor in this. They're not to me, because they're going to stand out like an out of tune instrument in a band. Group casting has to harmonize around magical principles. A Technocratic agent that can assist in Enlightened procedures might be able to infiltrate a Tradition Chantry, but their lack of belief in how the magic works for a given Tradition mage is going to single them out fast if they set themselves up to be part of a ritual casting session. If they're trained in how not to get caught, they're going to avoid entering into this equation, because it's going to blow their cover (because they're going to get caught reaaaaaly fast if suspected, mages are really good at telling if your lying, or if you have magic shielding your thoughts).<br /> <br /> But you seem to feel they really need to matter to this. I doubt we're going to come to some agreement on it, so either we can (a) not discuss the actual mechanic at all or (b) ignore that difference in how we build up the setting when we're playing, leading the discussion closer to the White Room. Hence the whole, &quot;White Room bad, narrative, narrative, setting, narrative&quot; routine is actually unhelpful at best. It turns into debates on what the narrative should be, rather than if the rule is actually good at what it's meant to do. We need to resort to the White Room if we're going to avoid that, because we don't have the time to explore the infinite variations of narrative possible.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, this assumes that World of Darkness\u2019 system is designed in such a way that what you would call \u2018fiat\u2019 is a last resort. That is not how Classic World of Darkness has been described by the authors or by most people you meet in the community. You are suggesting that a white room is a good way to judge balance when it practice it is not, since this game specifically incorporates numerous other elements of social and political combat that the authors indicate are an equally valid way to solve day to day situations at the table. It is not. This is not how the game itself has lent itself to being designed. Maybe at your table with lots of new and cool houserules, this would be the case.<br /> <br /> You are free to disagree and question the specific situations I give; on a personal level I\u2019m kind of not convinced by any of them without a good dunking of resources/effort to ensure that it doesn\u2019t happen (in which case, that\u2019s good! You should be rewarded for taking lots of precautions and using your resources effectively).<br /> <br /> But you\u2019ve constantly hammered home that somehow these narrative complications are invalid because it\u2019s \u201crule zero\u201d. Even when I point out to you that in every addition when the book is telling you about Cult or Acting in concert that these issues are present, you seemed to have glossed over them. So far, I think my examples and narrative considerations are a far more useful lens to see if this particular technique is imbalanced or not, since most tables do not exist in white rooms. They are things to consider that the book tells you to consider. At your table, maybe you don\u2019t want to focus on that. Or maybe you might not be away that it could serve as a potential issue. All tabletop discussion is subjective, but this rather odd insistence that a game specifically designed to encourage player\u2019s and STs to consider narrative consequences rather than murderhoboing should somehow have these integral elements stripped away because you don\u2019t consider it white room enough is honestly jarring.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you think ad hominem barbs and strawmen are doing anything but making you look even more dishonest... I have no idea what to tell you that shouldn't already be clear.</div> </div> </div> </div>This has genuinely been your argument thus far though. You\u2019re claiming that if we take narrative considerations into account that we would be stuck in stasis, our minds totally unable to wrap our heads around infinite possibilities and thus we could never have a conservation from that frame of reference. It\u2019s a reasonable position to assume that getting a lot of people together has a few more hangups that recruiting them to begin with. At a baseline, Mage the Ascension and World of Darkness take place in the real world but darker. Humans still have schedules, they still need to occupy space, they still need to be convinced. They\u2019re not automata. These are things you should consider. You are free to not consider them, just like you are free not to consider rules. You cannot, however, claim that it is invalid or unhelpful to bring up in a conversation. The majority of people who play this game aren\u2019t playing in White Rooms.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I don't need to retract something you're making up for whatever reason you seem to think is necessary.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It needed the nerf. I might be something of a rarity, but the cooperative casting rules are some of my favorites to bust on to show how powerful mages really are; esp. since you don't see it in play that much as most PC groups can't effectively use them due to wanted to run a multi-splat cabal instead of sharing enough beliefs to cast together.<br /> <br /> Five freebies for +5 successes to your ritual is <b>mind numbing</b> in terms of how good it is. There's no mechanical drawback, just the narrative issue of maintain your cult; but not having a cult with the old rules was a chump move because... how can you compete with +5 successes like that?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019m really getting bored by this weird obfuscation of your argument here. You\u2019re saying it needed a nerf. You\u2019re describing the Revised Cult background and the co-operative casting rules and waxing poetic about how good it is and how the narrative issue of maintaining it is seemingly swept under the rug because you failed to consider the actual narrative implications. Your current counter argument so far \u2018solving\u2019 these narrative issues is to straight up say they don\u2019t exist or to add lots of measures of security to ensure that is maintained in an optimum way then simultaneously saying that it\u2019s easy task. Not quite, my friend.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Your <b>only and single</b> counterargument is that it didn't need the nerf because narrative will constrain it; which I have responded to in depth. You're the one obfuscating the discussion with strawmen and lies.<br /> You haven't even mustered up a good argument. Countering &quot;but narrative!&quot; was easily done.<br /> <br /> And you appear like a troll, because you seem more interested in bashing the White Room (which wasn't even a significant issue in the discussion until you came in and made it one), than having an good faith chat about things.<br /> <br /> But sure, project more.<br /> <br /> Also remember that you're the one that decided to make this about each other, and not just the game, as well.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>My counter argument is simply that judging things from a white room perspective is not pragmatic in a game system like World of Darkness. I would like to remind you that you haven\u2019t actually gave a satisfying rebuttal to this. You have merely ran \u2018Rule Zero Fallacy\u2019 into the ground without considering that you\u2019re likely misapplying this term. You\u2019re also either unaware of what you\u2019ve wrote previously or you\u2019re hoping that I am unaware of what you\u2019ve wrote previously, please see above for evidence.<br /> <br /> If you think I\u2019m a troll because I\u2019m confused at how your argumentation seems to be at clear odds with the system in question despite your clear experience, I have no idea what to tell you. I\u2019m just very surprised that somehow these points and considerations have eluded to such an extent that you practically vilify them in favour of a platonic ideal that the system does not promise or intend to.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, besides all my experience with the game... my experience with online discussions is that White Room talk is by far not some newbie slanted thing. I have no idea where you'd get that impression, because over lots of forums, chatrooms, and so on, it's usually the old hands that jump into the White Room at the drop of a hat, not the new folks. Maybe the issue, giving the benefit of the doubt that you're actually scratching your head on this, is you need to read more online RPG talk?</div> </div> </div> </div>I have to say, on this forum and in the numerous RPG communities I frequent for this particular game system, it\u2019s usually newer players who are experimenting with the system that seem to lean toward White Room battles. I\u2019m not doubting that in other game systems more suited toward combat and specific mechanical inclinations that \u2018White Room\u2019 is more among the esoterica of grognards, but it\u2019s not something common in this game. I\u2019d recommend you\u2019d check this forum for these systems before automatically assuming one thing about a separate community and then misapplying it here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're. Making. This. Up.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've seen lots of people treat forums like the cliche of being new to a prison and beating up someone to prove you're tough. I've been told directly by a couple people that were trying to win a debate with me specifically to show they should be shown respect even though nobody was actually disrespecting them until their bad faith was obvious. You're giving me those vibes.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh, it's not at all about fame, it's about the number under the name that seems to set people off. I'm just the big number that happened to be here.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is what I mean by the weird mythology/roleplay scenario. I\u2019m not interested in it, you can rest assured there\u2019s no glory to be had here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First, never said there was a problem, just that narrative constraints are easier to game than mechanical restraints. Though if you'd like me to elaborate on the issues of why narrative granted traits bought with the same pool of points as traits that require limited mechanical resources to acquire I can do so easily. Or you could go to the VtM forum as ask &quot;Why was Generation being a Background a bad thing?&quot; if you want a lot of people to give the same answer.<br /> <br /> Second, no rules were bent or broken.<br /> <br /> Third, what cap would she be tweaking? There's no cap on how many dots in Backgrounds you can get during a session. There's only a cap on how many you can get in a single Background at a time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Check M20, it draws to an attention an optional rule for Revised where you must purchase backgrounds with XP. It\u2019s a possibility.<br /> Secondly, the system and game world are heavily intertwined and mostly grounded in an otherwise believable but darker world. There\u2019s rules and narrative decisions to how the world works, after all. You aren\u2019t beholden to them, just like you aren\u2019t beholden to every rule in the game (because pretty much each book tells you to invoke the golden rule if something makes sense), but by your metric if you\u2019re in a situation that doesn\u2019t make sense in the narrative of the world that the writers wrote then you\u2019re incorporating fiat.<br /> If the situation does make sense and everyone seems to agree with it, then\u2026what\u2019s the issue?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you mean XP, not it isn't negotiated.<br /> <br /> If you mean some vague concept of the experience of playing the game... it probably should be but it isn't nearly as much as you seem to think.</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019m guessing that you and I have very different expectations on a negotiated experience, since you seem very adamant that all conceptualisation of game balance and consideration begins and ends in the white room. And I, I hope you can understand, do not subscribe to that theory.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just saying it doesn't make it true. You have yet to give a single reason why nerfing acting in concert is a bad rule. I've already established that the one thing it supposedly cut out of the narrative tool kit (big guns bring big effects) is actually still firmly in place. You've offered no countered to my points about how moving it to a dice bonus instead of a success bonus actually helps highlight the narrative around it. You're just bitching about the White Room and tossing out half-baked assertions that never make a point.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That misogynistic and hurtful language is totally uncalled for.<br /> I\u2019ve given numerous reasons why the rule did not need a nerf and you\u2019ve swept them up and placed them in the \u2018narrative chamber pot\u2019 never to be seen again. You\u2019ve blatantly ignored my points to appeal to situations devoid of narrative context which most players are going to be occupying.<br /> My position is that Cult\u2019s(Revised) logistical issues that the book outlines for you in plain English is in it of itself a balancing feature. I think the fact those automatic successes don't count to cancelling botches means that in many respects it can become a liability that will cause a lot more serious damage if you misuse it. You haven\u2019t given me a convincing argument for why in the majority of situations this approach is invalid.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, just something I didn't feel like repeating because you want to cherry pick from things I said on the first page of the thread.</div> </div> </div> </div>So on the one hand you haven\u2019t said the things that I\u2019m supposedly \u2018accusing\u2019 you of, but on the other hand I\u2019m \u2018cherrypicking\u2019 things you\u2019ve said on the first page?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383561#post1383561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, right, Rule Zero fallacy again.</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019ve explained above why this isn\u2019t the rules Zero Fallacy.<br /> <br /> Honestly, the amount of vitriol here is really discouraging. I understand friendly debate can get heated, but you\u2019re seriously acting out of line here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26024-distant-choir","name":"Distant Choir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26024_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T16:27:39-07:00","id":1383682,"title":"","content":"I think it's not the problem.<br /> If there's a very huge ritual where's 1000 people involved, you should forget the rules and dice rolls and take everything in your, Storyteller's, hands. It's not the usual casting on the streets, it's an important plot event and it should be ended in a way that both you and your players consider more interesting.<br /> It would be very deamatic moment when thousands of christians gather to witness the real big M Miracle that WILL work. Or the whole tribe is involved in ritual to please the spirits for good harvest. Etc","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24212-mesmeroflife","name":"MesmerOfLife","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T18:04:33-07:00","id":1383699,"title":"","content":"*puts on mod hat*<br /> <br /> Guys, can we loosen it up a bit?<br /> <br /> Remember that the Ascension War. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> please don't say people are &quot;bitching&quot; about things even if you don't like their tone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T21:41:23-07:00","id":1383716,"title":"","content":"In an attempt to <s>derail</s>redirect the thread, I think it would be nice to have a consistent approach to \u201cgroup magic\u201d where Sleepers are concerned: <br /> <br /> whether it be large assemblies of acolytes as has thus far been discussed in this thread, <br /> <br /> or the matter of how Sleepers can be used as Instruments in a working (i.e., Management and Human Resources: should the willworker run into more difficulties the more people he tries to include? If everyone involved are acolytes that share the Willworker's Focus, can they be used to perform magick that they'd consider Coincidental even if the Reality Zone they're in considered it to be Vulgar?), <br /> <br /> or the question of what makes a Primal Venture (working theory: a significant portion of the people involved in the Venture must be Extraordinary Citizens, specifically ones who have bought into Hypereconomics; otherwise it isn't a <i>Primal</i> Venture), <br /> <br /> some guidelines as to what effect the activities of a Cult have on the local Reality Zone (theory: they can generate and sustain \u201cmythic threads\u201d that last for as long as they're around; but actually <i>shifting the Reality Zone</i> usually takes something more), <br /> <br /> some sense of how much of an impact small and large groups of Witnesses has on Paradox (IMHO, groups of Witnesses ought to intensify the problems with Paradox, and the larger the group the more intense the problems), <br /> <br /> and so on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-24T23:35:55-07:00","id":1383725,"title":"","content":"Uh. Wow. Long thread.<br /> <br /> But, I wanna actually respond to the very first post, because I think Captain Otter is...overlooking something very, very, very important in regards to the Mage 20th rule:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CaptOtter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals#post1382668\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Background on the issue: In 2nd and 3rd/Revised edition, the rules for &quot;Acting in Concert&quot; put forward that every 5 acolytes involved in an effect contribute an automatic success to the end result of the ritual. In M20, it's every 5 acolytes contributing a single die, <i>and</i> (here's the part I don't get) it's capped it at 5 additional dice.<br /> ...<br /> But this brings me to the rule at hand about Acolytes contributing to rituals. Switching it from automatic successes to dice doesn't make me feel one way or another--but capping it at a maximum benefit of five is inexplicable to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>In 2nd/Revised, the Cult background / Acolytes added an automatic success for every 5 Acolytes. <i>It also did those only up to 5 successes</i>. It added those successes to the <i>effect</i>. To the <i>END RESULT</i> of the ritual. The Cult background mentioned in Guide To The Traditions specifically mentions that it represents the Acolytes helping in said ritual:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As listed on p. 154 of Mage:<br /> The Ascension, a group of believing acolytes can<br /> add successes to the results of an Effect, if they<br /> participate and truly believe in your mage\u2019s power. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Add five successes to any<br /> rituals cast when the entire cult is gathered. </div> </div> </div>So it technically always was capped at 5 before as well, given the Cult writeup, and it <i>added to the end result</i>.<br /> Even if you ruled that you could get more than 5, it also only added to the end result.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now, here is the super important part about the 20th Cult background writeup and the change from automatgic success of the ritual, to dice:<br /> <br /> In Mage 20, the Cult background adds up to 5 dice to the <i>CASTERS DICE POOL</i>. That is, you get to roll up to 5 additional dice *per ritual roll*. Not the end result. Per Ritual roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As detailed under Allies, Assistants, and Cults and Acting<br /> in Concert (Chapter Ten, p. 532 and pp. 542-543), a group<br /> of assistants who share a common belief add to the caster\u2019s<br /> dice pool in a ritual cast with their help </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Cult Background adds one die to the caster\u2019s dice<br /> pool, up to a maximum of five dice. </div> </div> </div><br /> This isn't a Nerf. It's a huge <i>*boost*</i>.<br /> Double so with M20 going back to the pre-revised rules for using total successes of a roll for the aspects fo an effect, by default. In 20th, getting a group of acolytes together is more powerful than before.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 04-25-2020, 03:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-25T02:30:47-07:00","id":1383739,"title":"","content":"You're 8 Arete Mage, Harry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24212-mesmeroflife","name":"MesmerOfLife","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-25T03:40:36-07:00","id":1383746,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1382668-i-don-t-understand-the-purpose-of-m20-nerfing-acolyte-contributions-to-rituals?p=1383725#post1383725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So it technically always was capped at 5 before as well, given the Cult writeup, and it <i>added to the end result</i>.<br /> Even if you ruled that you could get more than 5, it also only added to the end result.</div> </div> </div> </div>All of this was actually covered already.<br /> <br /> The thing is that while Cult was always +5, the acting in concert rules weren't. If a five mage cabal that all follow the same magical principles and all had Cult 5 individually, they could lend their Cults to each other for +25 successes (and vulgar w/o witnesses effects would become coincidental) to any ritual rolls (and also less strict rules on ritual rolls back in earlier editions).<br /> <br /> As was explicitly noted in previous editions, you could have as many people helping as you could get together.<br /> <br /> When you can get to that level, the &quot;at the end&quot; aspect isn't that important. Being able to hit 26 successes minimum one on ritual roll is as many successes as most PC mages will even actually need for a spell. With PC level stuff, you're rarely going to be high difficulty, so getting at least one success is extremely likely.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This isn't a Nerf. It's a huge <i>*boost*</i>.<br /> Double so with M20 going back to the pre-revised rules for using total successes of a roll for the aspects fo an effect, by default. In 20th, getting a group of acolytes together is more powerful than before. </div> </div> </div>It's not bad, but the maximum potential is generally lower (esp. for PCs instead of powerhouse NPCs).<br /> <br /> The issue with calling it a boost is the risk of rolling as many times as you'd need to hit the same level of high successes. An mage rolling ~8 dice over 10 times has a significantly higher chance that once of the rolls fails or botches than a mage with ~3 dice rolling once or twice (and thus having the WP to spare for each roll just in case). This is especially risky with the &quot;more dice = more of your failed rolls are botches&quot; quirk of the system.<br /> <br /> Turning it into dice means one average an individual mage not pooling acolytes with others and so on has the potential to roll even better than just adding +5 successes at the end, but also a significant increase on the risks of the ritual exploding in their face (and their cult's faces); esp. again, with M20's more substantial ritual rules.<br /> <br /> Once you have multiple mages pooling their resources together, the math moves towards what got nerfed: the ability to gather lots and lots and lots of people together for huge automatic successes at the end.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-26T20:50:33-07:00","id":1384094,"title":"","content":"Thinking about the original idea here. Maybe it was nerfed so the Technocracy equivalent wouldn't get out of hand? Like if you had a whole international organization working on a &quot;ritual&quot;? Just to be clear though, I have no real opinion on the Nerf and feel that mages are still way ahead of the other supernaturals in terms of power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-26T23:31:06-07:00","id":1384112,"title":"","content":"It was needed because it was broken as he'll before. Every mid level syndicate mage got dozens of automatic successes on every primal utility working. I was in one game where we created multiple nodes in every city simply because it was easier to create nodes than to wait for the node to refill so you could use it again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/706-rickayelm","name":"rickayelm","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"76"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-27T01:00:07-07:00","id":1384121,"title":"","content":"That seems a bit extreme just because creating a Node is a Master level effect. That example at least has been a fairly long standing issue with Prime (and isn't really changed by Primal Utility) once it hits high levels.<br /> <br /> Even with the new rules, if your game is already at the point where you have a Prime Master, quintessence is no longer a scarce resource; I'd imagine this wouldn't be a non-viable even if harder/riskier option with the new rules as well.<br /> <br /> However much I think it's fair to put blame on other aspects of the system for contributing to that, it is sort of what I've been trying to say.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}